window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-cbse10-sci-01","kind":"practice","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"title":"Chemical Reactions and Equations · Practice","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","subject":"Science","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-001","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":1,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are combination reactions?\n(i) A magnesium ribbon burning in air to form magnesium oxide\n(ii) $H_2(g) + Cl_2(g) \\rightarrow 2HCl(g)$\n(iii) $Ca(OH)_2(aq) + CO_2(g) \\rightarrow CaCO_3(s) + H_2O(l)$\n(iv) $C(s) + O_2(g) \\rightarrow CO_2(g)$\n(v) $CH_4(g) + 2O_2(g) \\rightarrow CO_2(g) + 2H_2O(g)$\n(vi) $CaO(s) + H_2O(l) \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2(aq)$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A combination reaction forms one single product from two or more reactants.\n(i) Yes: Mg and $O_2$ give only MgO.\n(ii) Yes: the only product is HCl.\n(iii) No: it gives two products, $CaCO_3$ and $H_2O$.\n(iv) Yes: the only product is $CO_2$.\n(v) No: it gives $CO_2$ and $H_2O$. It is exothermic, but being exothermic does not make it a combination reaction.\n(vi) Yes: the only product is $Ca(OH)_2$.\nSo the count is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-002","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":2,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mg + O$_2$ → MgO is a skeletal chemical equation.\nReason (R): The number of oxygen atoms on the reactant side is not equal to that on the product side.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: an unbalanced equation written with formulae is called a skeletal equation.\nR is true: there are 2 O atoms on the left side and only 1 O atom on the right side.\nThe equation is skeletal precisely because the atoms are not equal on both sides, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-003","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":3,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the equation $CO(g) + 2H_2(g) \\rightarrow CH_3OH(l)$, the value '340 atm' is written above the arrow. It represents","image":"","options":["the amount of heat released in the reaction","the catalyst used in the reaction","the pressure at which the reaction is carried out","the mass of methanol formed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Reaction conditions such as temperature, pressure and catalyst can be written above and/or below the arrow.\nThe unit atm (atmosphere) is a unit of pressure.\nSo 340 atm is the pressure condition for the reaction.\nA catalyst would be named as a substance, not given as a pressure value.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-004","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":4,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Case: A white solid X is obtained by strongly heating limestone. When water is added to X, it reacts vigorously and releases a lot of heat, forming Y. A solution of Y is applied on a wall, and after about three days a thin shiny layer of Z forms on it.\nWhich option correctly identifies X, Y and Z and the type of reaction in which X changes to Y?","image":"","options":["X = $CaO$, Y = $Ca(OH)_2$, Z = $CaCO_3$; combination reaction","X = $CaO$, Y = $Ca(OH)_2$, Z = $CaCO_3$; decomposition reaction","X = $CaCO_3$, Y = $CaO$, Z = $Ca(OH)_2$; combination reaction","X = $Ca(OH)_2$, Y = $CaO$, Z = $CaCO_3$; displacement reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Heating limestone gives quick lime: $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$. So X = CaO.\nAdding water to it gives $CaO + H_2O \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2 + Heat$. So Y is slaked lime.\nThis step forms a single product from two reactants, so it is a combination reaction (and exothermic).\nOn the wall, $Ca(OH)_2$ reacts with $CO_2$ in air to form Z = $CaCO_3$, which gives the shine.\nOption 2 has the correct substances but the wrong reaction type.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-005","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":5,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A china dish containing white silver chloride is kept in sunlight for some time. The solid slowly turns grey. Which of the following correctly describes this change?","image":"","options":["A combination reaction in which silver chloride takes up oxygen from air and forms grey silver oxide","A decomposition reaction caused by light, in which silver chloride breaks down into grey silver metal and chlorine gas","A thermal decomposition reaction in which the heat of the sun changes silver chloride into silver oxide","A displacement reaction in which chlorine of the air displaces silver from silver chloride"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: $2AgCl(s) \\xrightarrow{Sunlight} 2Ag(s) + Cl_2(g)$.\nA single reactant breaks into simpler products, so it is a decomposition reaction.\nThe energy comes from light, not heat. The grey colour is due to silver metal, not an oxide.\nThis reaction, like the one for AgBr, is used in black and white photography.\nOxygen plays no role, so (1) and (3) fail. No element replaces silver, so (4) fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-006","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":6,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the reaction 2Cu + O$_2$ → 2CuO, copper is reduced.\nStatement II: In this reaction, copper gains oxygen.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct: copper combines with oxygen and forms CuO.\nStatement I is incorrect: gaining oxygen is oxidation, so copper is oxidised, not reduced.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-007","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":7,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the balanced equation 3Fe(s) + 4H$_2$O(g) → Fe$_3$O$_4$(s) + 4H$_2$(g).\nHow many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The total number of atoms on each side is 15.\n(ii) The symbol (g) with H$_2$O shows that water is used as steam.\n(iii) The total number of molecules/atoms (formula units) on the two sides is equal.\n(iv) 6Fe + 8H$_2$O → 2Fe$_3$O$_4$ + 8H$_2$ also has equal atoms on both sides, but hit-and-trial prefers the smallest whole-number coefficients.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: left side has 3 Fe + 8 H + 4 O = 15 atoms; right side has 7 (in Fe$_3$O$_4$) + 8 (in 4H$_2$) = 15 atoms.\n(ii) Correct: this reaction uses steam, so H$_2$O is marked (g).\n(iii) Incorrect: the left side has 3 + 4 = 7 units, while the right side has 1 + 4 = 5 units.\n(iv) Correct: doubling all coefficients keeps atoms equal, but the smallest whole numbers are used.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-008","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":8,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the reaction MnO2 + 4HCl → MnCl2 + 2H2O + Cl2.\nStatement I: In this reaction, HCl is oxidised and MnO2 is reduced.\nStatement II: HCl loses hydrogen to form Cl2, while MnO2 loses oxygen to form MnCl2.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Oxidation means gain of oxygen or loss of hydrogen. Reduction means loss of oxygen or gain of hydrogen.\nStatement II is correct: HCl loses hydrogen and becomes Cl2, and MnO2 loses its oxygen and becomes MnCl2.\nStatement I is correct: losing hydrogen means HCl is oxidised, and losing oxygen means MnO2 is reduced. This makes the reaction a redox reaction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-009","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":9,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following methods help to slow down rancidity of food containing fats and oils?\n(a) Adding antioxidants to the food\n(b) Storing the food in airtight containers\n(c) Flushing the food packet with nitrogen\n(d) Keeping the food in an open dish in a warm room\n(e) Filling the food packet with oxygen","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Helps: antioxidants prevent oxidation.\n(b) Helps: airtight containers cut off air and slow oxidation.\n(c) Helps: nitrogen pushes oxygen out of the packet.\n(d) Does not help: an open dish exposes the food to air, which speeds up oxidation.\n(e) Does not help: extra oxygen causes more oxidation.\nSo 3 methods slow rancidity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-010","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":10,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In Activity 1.10, sodium sulphate solution is mixed with barium chloride solution and a white substance that does not dissolve in water appears. Which substance is it?","image":"","options":["Sodium chloride","Barium sulphate","Barium chloride","Sodium sulphate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The reaction is $Na_2SO_4(aq) + BaCl_2(aq) \\rightarrow BaSO_4(s) + 2NaCl(aq)$.\nBarium sulphate is insoluble in water, so it separates out as a white precipitate.\nSodium chloride, the most tempting option, is the other product. It stays dissolved, which is why it carries the (aq) label.\nBarium chloride and sodium sulphate are the reactants, and both are already in solution.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-011","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":11,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student heats a green crystalline substance X in a dry boiling tube. The crystals first lose their green colour. On further heating, a solid residue Y remains, and two gases with the odour of burning sulphur escape. Which of the following correctly describes this experiment?","image":"","options":["Y is $FeO$, and the gases are $SO_2$ and $O_2$; the reaction is a combination reaction with air.","Y is $Fe_2O_3$, and the gases are $SO_2$ and $SO_3$; the reaction is a thermal decomposition.","Y is $Fe_2O_3$, and the gases are $SO_3$ and water vapour only; the reaction is a displacement reaction.","Y is $FeSO_4$, and the gases are $H_2S$ and $SO_2$; the reaction is exothermic."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"X is $FeSO_4 \\cdot 7H_2O$, the green ferrous sulphate crystals. It loses water first, which explains the colour change.\nIt then decomposes: $2FeSO_4 \\rightarrow Fe_2O_3 + SO_2 + SO_3$. So Y is ferric oxide, and the two sulphur oxides cause the burning-sulphur smell.\nA single reactant breaking down on heating is a thermal decomposition, not a combination or displacement reaction.\nIt needs a continuous supply of heat, so calling it exothermic is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-012","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":12,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the reaction ZnO + C → Zn + CO, which substance is reduced?","image":"","options":["ZnO","C","Zn","CO"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"ZnO loses its oxygen and becomes Zn, so ZnO is reduced.\nCarbon gains oxygen to form CO, so carbon is oxidised.\nZn and CO are products. We describe the reactants as oxidised or reduced, not the products.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-013","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":13,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Respiration is an exothermic process.\nReason (R): In the cells of our body, glucose combines with oxygen to form carbon dioxide and water, and energy is released.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: respiration releases energy, so it is exothermic.\nR is true: $C_6H_{12}O_6 + 6O_2 \\rightarrow 6CO_2 + 6H_2O$ + energy.\nThe energy released in this reaction is exactly why respiration is called exothermic, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-014","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":14,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student mixes two colourless aqueous solutions, X and Y. A yellow solid settles at the bottom. On filtering, the clear filtrate is found to contain potassium nitrate. What are X and Y, and what type of reaction occurred?","image":"","options":["Lead nitrate and potassium iodide; double displacement","Silver nitrate and sodium chloride; double displacement","Lead nitrate and potassium iodide; displacement","Barium chloride and potassium sulphate; double displacement"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A yellow precipitate together with $KNO_3$ in the filtrate points to $Pb(NO_3)_2 + 2KI \\rightarrow PbI_2 + 2KNO_3$.\nThe two compounds exchange ions, so the reaction is double displacement, not simple displacement.\n$AgNO_3$ with $NaCl$ gives a white precipitate and $NaNO_3$ in solution.\n$BaCl_2$ with $K_2SO_4$ gives white $BaSO_4$ and $KCl$ in solution.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-015","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":15,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Walls whitewashed with a solution of slaked lime develop a shiny finish two to three days after whitewashing.\nReason (R): Calcium hydroxide reacts slowly with carbon dioxide in air to form a thin layer of calcium carbonate on the walls.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The shiny finish appears only after two to three days, not immediately.\nR is true. $Ca(OH)_2(aq) + CO_2(g) \\rightarrow CaCO_3(s) + H_2O(l)$ takes place slowly with the $CO_2$ present in air.\nThe thin layer of calcium carbonate (the same compound as marble) is what gives the shine. The slow speed of this reaction explains the delay.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-016","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":16,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following involve a chemical change?\nSouring of milk left out in summer, rusting of an iron nail in humid air, fermentation of grapes, digestion of food, respiration, melting of ice, dissolving sugar in water","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Souring of milk: chemical, because new substances form.\nRusting of iron: chemical.\nFermentation of grapes: chemical.\nDigestion of food: chemical.\nRespiration: chemical.\nMelting of ice and dissolving sugar in water are physical changes, because the identity of the substance does not change.\nTotal chemical changes = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-017","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":17,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student heats about 1 g of shiny brown copper powder in a china dish over a burner. After some time, the surface of the powder becomes coated with a black substance. What is the black substance, and what has happened to copper?","image":"","options":["Copper(II) oxide; copper has been oxidised","Copper(II) oxide; copper has been reduced","Copper sulphate; copper has been oxidised","Carbon (soot) from the flame; copper is unchanged"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"When copper is heated in air, it combines with oxygen: $2Cu + O_2 \\rightarrow 2CuO$.\nThe black coating is copper(II) oxide, CuO.\nCopper gains oxygen, so copper is oxidised.\nOption (2) is wrong because reduction is the loss of oxygen, not the gain of it.\nCopper sulphate needs sulphur, and none is present here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-018","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":18,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student did the following at room temperature (25 °C):\nBeaker B: two spatulas of ammonium nitrate were stirred into 25 mL water. The temperature fell to 18 °C.\nBeaker C: two spatulas of anhydrous copper sulphate were stirred into 25 mL water. The temperature rose to 31 °C.\nBeaker D: fine iron filings were stirred into copper sulphate solution. The temperature rose slightly and the blue colour faded.\nHow many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The change in beaker B absorbs heat from the water.\n(ii) Heat is released when anhydrous copper sulphate is added to water in beaker C.\n(iii) In beaker D, an exothermic displacement reaction takes place in which iron displaces copper.\n(iv) The fall in temperature in beaker B shows that heat was given out to the surroundings.\n(v) Mixing barium hydroxide with ammonium chloride would show a temperature change similar to that in beaker C.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the temperature fell, so heat was absorbed from the water. The change is endothermic.\n(ii) Correct: the temperature rose, so heat was released. The change is exothermic.\n(iii) Correct: Fe + CuSO$_4$ → FeSO$_4$ + Cu is a displacement reaction. The rise in temperature shows that heat is released.\n(iv) Incorrect: a fall in temperature means heat was taken in, not given out.\n(v) Incorrect: Ba(OH)$_2$ + NH$_4$Cl is endothermic and turns cold. It behaves like beaker B, not beaker C.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-019","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":19,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the reaction $MnO_2 + 4HCl \\rightarrow MnCl_2 + 2H_2O + Cl_2$, HCl gets reduced.\nReason (R): A substance that loses hydrogen during a reaction is said to be oxidised.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true: loss of hydrogen (or gain of oxygen) is oxidation.\nIn this reaction HCl loses hydrogen and becomes $Cl_2$. So HCl is oxidised, not reduced, and A is false.\n$MnO_2$ loses oxygen, so it is the substance that is reduced.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-020","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":20,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Dilute sulphuric acid is added to zinc granules in a conical flask. Which pair of observations is expected?","image":"","options":["Bubbles of oxygen form and the flask becomes cold.","No bubbles form, but the zinc turns blue.","Bubbles of chlorine form and the flask becomes hot.","Bubbles of hydrogen form around the granules and the flask becomes warm."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Zinc reacts with dilute sulphuric acid to form zinc sulphate and hydrogen gas.\nHydrogen bubbles appear around the zinc granules.\nThe flask feels warm because heat is released during the reaction.\nOxygen is not produced. Chlorine cannot form because the acid contains no chlorine.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-021","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":21,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following involve a chemical change?\n(i) Milk turning sour when kept at room temperature in summer\n(ii) An iron nail rusting in humid air\n(iii) Ice melting in a glass\n(iv) Fermentation of grapes\n(v) Sugar dissolving in water\n(vi) Digestion of food in our body\n(vii) Respiration","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Chemical: souring of milk forms new substances.\n(ii) Chemical: rust is a new substance formed on iron.\n(iii) Physical: melting is only a change of state, and water remains water.\n(iv) Chemical: fermentation changes the identity of the substances in grapes.\n(v) Physical: sugar can be recovered by evaporating the water.\n(vi) Chemical: food is broken down into simpler substances.\n(vii) Chemical: glucose reacts with oxygen to give $CO_2$, water and energy.\nTotal chemical changes = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-022","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":22,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student writes the photosynthesis equation as $6CO_2(aq) + 12H_2O(l) \\rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6(aq) + 6O_2(aq) + 6H_2O(l)$, with 'Sunlight' written above the arrow and 'Chlorophyll' below it. Which statement about this equation is correct?","image":"","options":["Sunlight and chlorophyll are reactants and should be written on the left-hand side with plus signs","The equation is unbalanced because water appears on both sides","The equation is balanced, and sunlight and chlorophyll only indicate the conditions needed for the reaction","Chlorophyll is used up in the reaction and should appear with a coefficient"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Count the atoms on the left: C = 6, H = 24, O = 12 + 12 = 24.\nCount the atoms on the right: C = 6, H = 12 + 12 = 24, O = 6 + 12 + 6 = 24.\nThe equation is balanced, even though water appears on both sides.\nSunlight and chlorophyll are written on the arrow as conditions. They are neither reactants nor products, so they get no coefficients.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-023","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":23,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student places a clean zinc strip in beaker P containing copper sulphate solution. She places a clean copper strip in beaker Q containing zinc sulphate solution. After some time, how many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The blue colour of the solution in beaker P fades.\n(ii) A reddish-brown deposit forms on the zinc strip in beaker P.\n(iii) No noticeable change occurs in beaker Q.\n(iv) The reaction in beaker P is a double displacement reaction.","image":"","options":["1","2","4","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Zinc is more reactive than copper, so Zn + CuSO4 → ZnSO4 + Cu takes place in P.\n(i) Correct: blue CuSO4 is used up, and ZnSO4 is colourless.\n(ii) Correct: displaced copper deposits on the zinc strip.\n(iii) Correct: copper is less reactive, so it cannot displace zinc from ZnSO4.\n(iv) Incorrect: one element displaces another, so this is a single displacement. It is not an exchange of ions.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-024","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":24,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student heats about 2 g of green ferrous sulphate crystals ($FeSO_4 \\cdot 7H_2O$) in a dry boiling tube. Consider the following statements about this activity:\n(i) The crystals first lose their water of crystallisation, and their colour changes.\n(ii) The anhydrous salt then decomposes into one solid product and two gaseous products.\n(iii) A smell like that of burning sulphur is noticed.\n(iv) The solid residue left behind is ferrous oxide.\n(v) The reaction is a combination reaction because three products combine.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the hydrated crystals lose water on heating, and the green colour changes.\n(ii) Correct: $2FeSO_4 \\rightarrow Fe_2O_3(s) + SO_2(g) + SO_3(g)$ gives one solid and two gases.\n(iii) Correct: the oxides of sulphur give the odour of burning sulphur.\n(iv) Incorrect: the residue is $Fe_2O_3$, which is ferric oxide, not ferrous oxide.\n(v) Incorrect: a single reactant breaks down, so this is a decomposition reaction.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-025","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":25,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In Activity 1.7, water is electrolysed using carbon electrodes connected to a 6 V battery. The gases are collected in two inverted test tubes.\nAssertion (A): The gas collected in the test tube with the larger volume burns with a pop sound when a burning candle is brought near it.\nReason (R): During electrolysis of water, oxygen is produced in twice the volume of hydrogen.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The reaction is $2H_2O(l) \\rightarrow 2H_2(g) + O_2(g)$, so hydrogen and oxygen form in a 2 : 1 volume ratio.\nA is true: the larger volume is hydrogen, which burns with a pop sound.\nR is false: hydrogen, not oxygen, is produced in double the volume.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-026","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":26,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"When potassium iodide solution is added to lead nitrate solution, a precipitate forms. What are its colour and identity?","image":"","options":["White, potassium nitrate","Yellow, lead iodide","Yellow, potassium nitrate","Brown, lead iodide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The reaction is $Pb(NO_3)_2(aq) + 2KI(aq) \\rightarrow PbI_2(s) + 2KNO_3(aq)$.\nLead iodide is insoluble and separates out as a yellow precipitate.\nPotassium nitrate remains dissolved in the solution, so it cannot be the precipitate.\nBrown is the colour of the $NO_2$ fumes seen when solid lead nitrate is heated, not of this precipitate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-027","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":27,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (a species in a reaction, balanced with the smallest whole-number coefficients) with List-II (its coefficient).\nList-I:\n(a) NH$_3$ in N$_2$ + H$_2$ → NH$_3$\n(b) O$_2$ in H$_2$S + O$_2$ → H$_2$O + SO$_2$\n(c) H$_2$ in Fe + H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + H$_2$\n(d) Ca(OH)$_2$ in HNO$_3$ + Ca(OH)$_2$ → Ca(NO$_3$)$_2$ + H$_2$O\nList-II:\n(p) 1\n(q) 2\n(r) 3\n(s) 4","image":"","options":["(a)-q, (b)-r, (c)-s, (d)-p","(a)-r, (b)-q, (c)-s, (d)-p","(a)-q, (b)-s, (c)-r, (d)-p","(a)-q, (b)-r, (c)-p, (d)-s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) N$_2$ + 3H$_2$ → 2NH$_3$, so NH$_3$ has coefficient 2 → q.\n(b) 2H$_2$S + 3O$_2$ → 2H$_2$O + 2SO$_2$, so O$_2$ has coefficient 3 → r.\n(c) 3Fe + 4H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + 4H$_2$, so H$_2$ has coefficient 4 → s.\n(d) 2HNO$_3$ + Ca(OH)$_2$ → Ca(NO$_3$)$_2$ + 2H$_2$O, so Ca(OH)$_2$ has coefficient 1 → p.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-028","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":28,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which two signs of a chemical reaction are shown when colourless solutions of lead nitrate and potassium iodide are mixed?","image":"","options":["Evolution of a gas and change in temperature","Change in colour and change in state","Change in temperature and evolution of a gas","Evolution of a gas and change in colour"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Two colourless solutions are mixed and a yellow solid appears.\nThe appearance of yellow colour is a change in colour.\nA solid (precipitate) forming from liquids (solutions) is a change in state.\nNo gas is evolved in this reaction, so every option that includes gas evolution is ruled out.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-029","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":29,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student slowly adds water to a small amount of quick lime in a beaker and then touches the beaker. Which of the following correctly describes what the student observes and the nature of the reaction?","image":"","options":["The beaker feels cold, so the reaction is endothermic","The beaker becomes hot, so the reaction is exothermic","There is no change in temperature, so no reaction takes place","The beaker feels cold, so the reaction is exothermic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Quick lime (CaO) reacts vigorously with water and forms slaked lime, $Ca(OH)_2$.\nThis reaction releases a large amount of heat, so the beaker becomes hot.\nA reaction that gives out heat along with its products is exothermic.\nThe beaker does not feel cold, because no heat is absorbed from the surroundings.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-030","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":30,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a zinc granule is placed in blue copper sulphate solution, a reddish-brown deposit forms on the zinc.\nReason (R): Zinc is more reactive than copper and displaces it from its compound.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The reaction is $Zn(s) + CuSO_4(aq) \\rightarrow ZnSO_4(aq) + Cu(s)$.\nA is true: the copper that is set free deposits on the zinc as a reddish-brown layer.\nR is true: NCERT states that zinc is more reactive than copper.\nR explains A, because displacement happens only when the free metal is more reactive than the metal in the compound.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-031","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":31,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A magnesium ribbon is rubbed with sandpaper before it is burnt in air.\nReason (R): Rubbing removes the layer of magnesium oxide on the surface of the ribbon, which would otherwise hinder burning.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in Activity 1.1 the ribbon is cleaned with sandpaper before burning.\nR is true: magnesium kept in air slowly gets coated with a layer of magnesium oxide, and this layer slows down burning.\nSandpaper removes the coating and exposes fresh metal, so the ribbon burns readily. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-032","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":32,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the electrolysis of water set-up (carbon electrodes, 6 V battery, gases collected in inverted test tubes), which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Equal volumes of gas collect in the two test tubes","A few drops of dilute sulphuric acid are added to the water in the mug","The energy needed to break down water is supplied in the form of light","The breaking down of water here is a combination reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Option 1 is wrong: the volume of hydrogen collected is double the volume of oxygen.\nOption 2 is correct: the activity adds a few drops of dilute H2SO4 to the water.\nOption 3 is wrong: the energy is supplied as electricity, not light.\nOption 4 is wrong: one reactant gives two products, so this is a decomposition reaction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-033","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":33,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following reactions are redox reactions, judged by gain or loss of oxygen or hydrogen?\n(i) 2Cu + O$_2$ → 2CuO\n(ii) CuO + H$_2$ → Cu + H$_2$O\n(iii) ZnO + C → Zn + CO\n(iv) Na$_2$SO$_4$ + BaCl$_2$ → BaSO$_4$ + 2NaCl\n(v) CaCO$_3$ → CaO + CO$_2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Redox: Cu gains oxygen and O$_2$ is used up as the oxidising reactant.\n(ii) Redox: CuO loses oxygen while H$_2$ gains oxygen.\n(iii) Redox: ZnO loses oxygen while C gains oxygen.\n(iv) Not redox: ions are only exchanged, so this is double displacement.\n(v) Not redox: this is thermal decomposition, and no substance gains or loses oxygen in the redox sense.\nThree reactions are redox.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-034","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":34,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The decomposition of vegetable matter into compost is an exothermic process.\nReason (R): During composting, the vegetable matter absorbs heat from its surroundings.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT lists the decomposition of vegetable matter into compost as an example of an exothermic reaction, because heat is given out. This is why a compost heap feels warm.\nR is false. Absorbing heat from the surroundings describes an endothermic change. In composting, heat is released, not absorbed.\nSo A is correct and R is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-035","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":35,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the hit-and-trial method of balancing equations is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The formulae of reactants and products are not changed while balancing.","It is convenient to start with the compound that has the maximum number of atoms.","While balancing Fe + H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + H$_2$, oxygen is balanced first.","The largest possible whole-number coefficients are chosen so that the equation is clearly balanced."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: each formula is boxed and its contents are never changed.\n(2) Correct: balancing usually begins with the compound containing the most atoms.\n(3) Correct: Fe$_3$O$_4$ has the most atoms, and oxygen has the most atoms within it, so oxygen is balanced first.\n(4) Incorrect: the method uses the smallest whole-number coefficients, not the largest.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-036","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":36,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Gas that burns with a pop sound in the electrolysis of water\n(b) Gas that makes a burning candle burn more brightly\n(c) Substance that gives the grey colour when AgCl is kept in sunlight\n(d) Element released along with silver when AgBr is exposed to light\nList-II\n(i) Silver\n(ii) Hydrogen\n(iii) Bromine\n(iv) Oxygen","image":"","options":["a-(ii), b-(iv), c-(i), d-(iii)","a-(iv), b-(ii), c-(i), d-(iii)","a-(ii), b-(iv), c-(iii), d-(i)","a-(iv), b-(ii), c-(iii), d-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Hydrogen is combustible and burns with a pop: (ii).\n(b) Oxygen supports burning, so the candle glows brighter: (iv).\n(c) Silver metal formed from AgCl gives the grey colour: (i).\n(d) $2AgBr \\rightarrow 2Ag + Br_2$, so bromine is released: (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-037","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":37,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (type of reaction).\nList-I\n(A) $Zn + CuSO_4 \\rightarrow ZnSO_4 + Cu$\n(B) $Na_2SO_4 + BaCl_2 \\rightarrow BaSO_4 + 2NaCl$\n(C) $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$\n(D) $CaO + H_2O \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2$\nList-II\n(i) Combination\n(ii) Decomposition\n(iii) Displacement\n(iv) Double displacement","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A: the element Zn displaces Cu from its compound, so it is displacement (iii).\nB: $Ba^{2+}$ and $Na^+$ exchange their anions, so it is double displacement (iv).\nC: a single reactant breaks into simpler products, so it is decomposition (ii).\nD: two reactants form a single product, so it is combination (i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-038","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":38,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (energy change).\nList-I\n(a) $2AgBr \\rightarrow 2Ag + Br_2$\n(b) $2H_2O \\rightarrow 2H_2 + O_2$ (Activity with carbon electrodes and a 6 V battery)\n(c) $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$\n(d) $CH_4 + 2O_2 \\rightarrow CO_2 + 2H_2O$\nList-II\n(i) Energy absorbed as electricity\n(ii) Energy absorbed as heat\n(iii) Energy released as heat\n(iv) Energy absorbed as light","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Silver bromide decomposes in sunlight, so it absorbs light energy: (iv).\n(b) Water breaks down when an electric current is passed, so it absorbs electrical energy: (i).\n(c) Limestone decomposes on heating, so it absorbs heat: (ii).\n(d) Burning of natural gas gives out heat: (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-039","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":39,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about state symbols and reaction conditions in chemical equations:\n(i) In $3Fe(s) + 4H_2O(g) \\rightarrow Fe_3O_4(s) + 4H_2(g)$, the symbol (g) with water shows that water reacts in the form of steam.\n(ii) In the equation for making methanol from $CO(g)$ and $H_2(g)$, the '340 atm' written on the arrow is a reaction condition, not a reactant.\n(iii) In the photosynthesis equation, sunlight and chlorophyll are written on the arrow because they are reactants used up in the reaction.\n(iv) The symbol (aq) means that the substance is a pure liquid.\n(v) In $NaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) \\rightarrow NaCl(aq) + H_2O(l)$, NaCl(aq) means that sodium chloride is present as a solution in water.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT writes $H_2O(g)$ in this reaction to show that steam is used.\n(ii) Correct: conditions such as temperature, pressure and catalyst are written above or below the arrow. They are not reactants.\n(iii) Incorrect: sunlight and chlorophyll are conditions (energy source and pigment). They are not reactants that appear on the LHS.\n(iv) Incorrect: (aq) means the substance is dissolved in water. A pure liquid is shown by (l).\n(v) Correct: NaCl stays dissolved in water, so it is written as NaCl(aq).\nSo three statements, (i), (ii) and (v), are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-040","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":40,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student performs Activity 1.3 using zinc granules and dilute hydrochloric acid. The zinc chloride formed stays dissolved in water. Which equation represents this reaction correctly, with balanced atoms and correct state symbols?","image":"","options":["Zn(s) + HCl(aq) → ZnCl(aq) + H$_2$(g)","Zn(s) + 2HCl(aq) → ZnCl$_2$(s) + H$_2$(l)","Zn(s) + 2HCl(aq) → ZnCl$_2$(aq) + H$_2$(g)","Zn(s) + 2HCl(aq) → ZnCl$_2$(aq) + 2H(g)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Products: zinc chloride (ZnCl$_2$, dissolved, so aq) and hydrogen gas (H$_2$, g).\nBalance: 2HCl provides 2 H and 2 Cl, matching ZnCl$_2$ + H$_2$.\n(1) uses the wrong formula ZnCl and is unbalanced.\n(2) gives wrong states: ZnCl$_2$ is in solution and hydrogen is a gas.\n(4) writes hydrogen as separate atoms instead of H$_2$ molecules.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-041","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":41,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student adds water slowly to a small amount of quick lime in a beaker and finds that the beaker becomes hot. Which of the following equations correctly shows the reaction and its type?","image":"","options":["$CaO(s) + H_2O(l) \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2(aq)$ + Heat; combination reaction","$CaCO_3(s) \\rightarrow CaO(s) + CO_2(g)$; decomposition reaction","$CaO(s) + H_2O(l) \\rightarrow CaCO_3(s) + H_2(g)$; displacement reaction","$Ca(OH)_2(aq) + CO_2(g) \\rightarrow CaCO_3(s) + H_2O(l)$; combination reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Quick lime is calcium oxide (CaO). It reacts vigorously with water to form slaked lime, $Ca(OH)_2$, and gives out a large amount of heat.\nTwo reactants form a single product, so this is a combination reaction. It is also exothermic.\nOption (2) is the decomposition of limestone. That reaction makes quick lime but does not use it.\nOption (4) is the whitewashing reaction. It gives two products, so it is not a combination reaction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-042","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":42,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are usually written above or below the arrow in a chemical equation as reaction conditions?\nTemperature, pressure, catalyst, sunlight, coefficient of a product, physical state of a reactant","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Temperature, pressure and catalyst are reaction conditions, so they go on the arrow.\nSunlight is also a condition: it is written on the arrow in the photosynthesis equation.\nThe coefficient of a product is written before its formula, not on the arrow.\nThe physical state is written next to the formula, as in (s) or (l).\nThat gives 4 items.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-043","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":43,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about displacement and double displacement reactions is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In $Fe + CuSO_4 \\rightarrow FeSO_4 + Cu$, an element replaces another element from its compound, so it is a displacement reaction.","In $AgNO_3 + NaCl \\rightarrow AgCl + NaNO_3$, ions are exchanged and a white precipitate of silver chloride forms.","Every double displacement reaction must produce a precipitate.","In a double displacement reaction, two compounds react to give two new compounds."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: free iron displaces copper from its compound.\n(2) Correct: $Ag^+$ and $Na^+$ exchange their anions, giving insoluble white $AgCl$.\n(3) Incorrect: the neutralisation $NaOH + HCl \\rightarrow NaCl + H_2O$ is a double displacement reaction, yet it forms water and dissolved $NaCl$ with no precipitate.\n(4) Correct: this is the general pattern of a double displacement reaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-044","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":44,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Decomposition reactions are considered to be the opposite of combination reactions.\nReason (R): In a decomposition reaction a single reactant breaks into two or more simpler products, whereas in a combination reaction two or more reactants form a single product.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT describes decomposition reactions as opposite to combination reactions.\nR is true: decomposition is one reactant giving several products, while combination is several reactants giving one product. An example is $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$ versus $C + O_2 \\rightarrow CO_2$.\nThis reversed pattern is exactly why the two types are called opposites, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-045","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":45,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following observations indicate that a chemical reaction has taken place?\n(i) Brown fumes appear when lead nitrate powder is heated\n(ii) The blue colour of copper sulphate solution fades when iron nails are kept in it\n(iii) A beaker becomes hot when water is added to quick lime\n(iv) Ice melts into water when kept at room temperature\n(v) A yellow precipitate forms when lead nitrate and potassium iodide solutions are mixed","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: NO2 gas is evolved, which is a new substance.\n(ii) Yes: the colour changes because iron displaces copper.\n(iii) Yes: the temperature rises because the reaction is exothermic.\n(iv) No: melting is only a physical change of state. No new substance is formed.\n(v) Yes: a new insoluble substance (PbI2) is formed.\nSo 4 observations indicate a chemical reaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-046","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":46,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are double displacement reactions?\n(i) $Pb(NO_3)_2 + 2KI \\rightarrow PbI_2 + 2KNO_3$\n(ii) $NaCl + AgNO_3 \\rightarrow AgCl + NaNO_3$\n(iii) $Fe + CuSO_4 \\rightarrow FeSO_4 + Cu$\n(iv) $NaOH + HCl \\rightarrow NaCl + H_2O$\n(v) $2AgCl \\rightarrow 2Ag + Cl_2$\n(vi) $BaCl_2 + H_2SO_4 \\rightarrow BaSO_4 + 2HCl$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Double displacement: ions are exchanged and yellow $PbI_2$ precipitates.\n(ii) Double displacement: ions are exchanged and white $AgCl$ forms.\n(iii) Not double displacement: a single element, Fe, displaces Cu, so it is displacement.\n(iv) Double displacement: ions are exchanged in this neutralisation.\n(v) Not double displacement: this is decomposition by light.\n(vi) Double displacement: ions are exchanged and $BaSO_4$ forms.\nSo the count is 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-047","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":47,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"While heating ferrous sulphate crystals in a dry boiling tube (Activity 1.5), a characteristic smell is noticed. This smell is like that of:","image":"","options":["Rotten eggs","Burning sulphur","Vinegar","Ammonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Heated ferrous sulphate decomposes into $Fe_2O_3$, $SO_2$ and $SO_3$.\nThe oxides of sulphur that escape as gases give the smell of burning sulphur, as noted in NCERT.\nA rotten-egg smell is typical of hydrogen sulphide, which is not formed in this reaction.\nNo vinegar-like or ammonia-like substance is produced either.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-048","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":48,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A copper vessel left exposed to moist air for a long time develops a coating. What colour is this coating?","image":"","options":["Reddish brown","Black","Green","White"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT gives three examples of corrosion: reddish-brown rust on iron, a black coating on silver and a green coating on copper.\nSo a copper vessel corroded by moist air turns green.\nReddish brown is tempting, but that is the colour of rust, which forms only on iron.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-049","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":49,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A few zinc granules are placed in a conical flask and dilute sulphuric acid is added. Consider these statements:\n(i) Bubbles of hydrogen gas form around the zinc granules.\n(ii) The flask feels warm, which shows a change in temperature.\n(iii) The equation $Zn + H_2SO_4 \\rightarrow ZnSO_4 + H_2$ is a skeletal equation, because it needs coefficients to be balanced.\n(iv) Both the evolution of gas and the change in temperature are evidence that a chemical reaction has taken place.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: zinc reacts with dilute $H_2SO_4$ and releases hydrogen gas.\n(ii) Correct: the reaction gives out heat, so the flask becomes warm.\n(iii) Incorrect: each side has Zn 1, H 2, S 1 and O 4. The equation is already balanced as written, so it is not skeletal.\n(iv) Correct: evolution of a gas and a change in temperature are both signs of a chemical reaction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-050","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":50,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following reactions are both combination reactions and exothermic reactions?\n(i) $C(s) + O_2(g) \\rightarrow CO_2(g)$\n(ii) $2H_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightarrow 2H_2O(l)$\n(iii) $CaO(s) + H_2O(l) \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2(aq)$ + heat\n(iv) $CaCO_3(s) \\rightarrow CaO(s) + CO_2(g)$ (on heating)\n(v) $CH_4(g) + 2O_2(g) \\rightarrow CO_2(g) + 2H_2O(g)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: burning of coal gives a single product and releases heat.\n(ii) Yes: formation of water gives a single product and releases heat.\n(iii) Yes: quick lime and water give slaked lime and a lot of heat.\n(iv) No: this is a thermal decomposition and needs heat (endothermic).\n(v) No: burning of natural gas is exothermic, but it gives two products, so it is not a combination reaction.\nThree reactions qualify.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-051","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":51,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Hydrogen sulphide burns in air to form water and sulphur dioxide:\nH$_2$S + O$_2$ → H$_2$O + SO$_2$\nWhen this is balanced using the smallest whole-number coefficients, what is the sum of all the coefficients?","image":"","options":["7","8","9","11"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sulphur: keep 1 H$_2$S and 1 SO$_2$. Hydrogen is then also balanced using 1 H$_2$O.\nOxygen: the right side has 2 + 1 = 3 O atoms. This would need 1.5 O$_2$.\nTo get whole numbers, double every coefficient: 2H$_2$S + 3O$_2$ → 2H$_2$O + 2SO$_2$.\nCheck: H 4 = 4, S 2 = 2, O 6 = 6.\nSum of coefficients = 2 + 3 + 2 + 2 = 9.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-052","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":52,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Case: A student performs part of the group activity at a room temperature of 25 °C and records the following observations.\nBeaker B (water + ammonium nitrate): final temperature 18 °C\nBeaker C (water + anhydrous copper sulphate): final temperature 32 °C\nBeaker D (copper sulphate solution + fine iron filings): final temperature 27 °C, and the blue colour fades\nWhich conclusion drawn by the student is correct?","image":"","options":["Beaker D shows an endothermic displacement reaction because the colour fades","Beakers B and C are both exothermic because the temperature changes in both","Beaker D shows an exothermic displacement reaction in which iron displaces copper from copper sulphate","Beaker B shows an exothermic change because ammonium nitrate dissolves completely"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"B: the temperature falls from 25 °C to 18 °C, so heat is absorbed and the change is endothermic. Options (2) and (4) are therefore wrong.\nC: the temperature rises, so the change is exothermic.\nD: the temperature rises, so the reaction is exothermic. Fe + $CuSO_4 \\rightarrow FeSO_4$ + Cu is a displacement reaction, and the blue colour fades as $CuSO_4$ is used up.\nA fading colour does not tell us the reaction is endothermic, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-053","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":53,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Manufacturers flush packets of potato chips with nitrogen gas before sealing them.\nReason (R): Nitrogen reacts with the oil present in the chips and forms a protective layer that stops the oil from reacting with oxygen.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that chip packets are flushed with a gas such as nitrogen to prevent the chips from getting oxidised.\nR is false. Nitrogen does not react with the oil or form a coating. It simply replaces the air (oxygen) inside the packet, so the fats and oils cannot be oxidised and turn rancid.\nPreventing rancidity works by keeping oxygen away. It is not a chemical reaction with nitrogen.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-054","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":54,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the balanced equation for the thermal decomposition of lead nitrate, what is the ratio of molecules of nitrogen dioxide to molecules of oxygen formed, and how many gas molecules in total are released from 2 formula units of lead nitrate?","image":"","options":["4 : 1 ; 5","2 : 1 ; 3","4 : 1 ; 4","1 : 4 ; 5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Balanced equation: $2Pb(NO_3)_2 \\rightarrow 2PbO + 4NO_2 + O_2$.\nGaseous products: 4 molecules of $NO_2$ and 1 molecule of $O_2$, giving a ratio of 4 : 1.\nTotal gas molecules = 4 + 1 = 5. $PbO$ is a solid, so it is not counted.\nOption (3) misses the $O_2$ molecule, and option (4) reverses the ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-055","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":55,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The thermal decomposition $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$ is the exact reverse of the combination reaction $CaO + H_2O \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2$.\nStatement II: In the balanced equation for the thermal decomposition of lead nitrate, four molecules of nitrogen dioxide are formed for every molecule of oxygen formed.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Decomposition is the opposite of combination, but the exact reverse of $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$ is $CaO + CO_2 \\rightarrow CaCO_3$. The reaction $CaO + H_2O \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2$ forms slaked lime, not limestone.\nStatement II is correct. The balanced equation is $2Pb(NO_3)_2 \\rightarrow 2PbO + 4NO_2 + O_2$, so $NO_2$ and $O_2$ form in the ratio 4 : 1.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-056","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":56,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mg + O$_2$ → MgO is a skeletal chemical equation because it does not show equal mass on both sides.\nStatement II: In this equation, the number of magnesium atoms on the LHS differs from that on the RHS.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct, but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Count the atoms. Mg: LHS 1, RHS 1, so Mg is balanced. O: LHS 2, RHS 1, so O is unbalanced.\nBecause the oxygen atoms are unequal, the mass is not the same on both sides. The equation is therefore skeletal, and Statement I is correct.\nStatement II is incorrect: the magnesium atoms are equal. Only oxygen is unbalanced.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-057","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":57,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Aluminium reacts with copper chloride solution to give aluminium chloride and copper. Which of the following is the correctly balanced equation for this reaction?","image":"","options":["$Al + CuCl_2 \\rightarrow AlCl_3 + Cu$","$2Al + 3CuCl_2 \\rightarrow 2AlCl_3 + 3Cu$","$2Al + CuCl_2 \\rightarrow 2AlCl + Cu$","$3Al + 2CuCl_2 \\rightarrow 3AlCl_3 + 2Cu$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aluminium chloride is $AlCl_3$, so we balance chlorine first: 3 $CuCl_2$ give 6 Cl, which matches 2 $AlCl_3$.\nThis gives 2 Al on the left and 3 Cu on the right.\nCheck: Al 2 = 2, Cu 3 = 3, Cl 6 = 6.\nOption (1) is unbalanced in Cl. Option (3) changes the formula of aluminium chloride, which is not allowed. Option (4) has 9 Cl on the right and only 4 on the left.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-058","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":58,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In a blast furnace, iron(III) oxide reacts with carbon monoxide to give iron and carbon dioxide:\nFe2O3 + CO → Fe + CO2 (unbalanced)\nWhen the equation is balanced using the smallest whole-number coefficients, what is the sum of all the coefficients, and which substance is oxidised?","image":"","options":["9; CO is oxidised","9; Fe2O3 is oxidised","7; CO is oxidised","5; CO is oxidised"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Balancing step by step:\n- Fe: put 2 before Fe.\n- O: Fe2O3 gives up 3 oxygen atoms, so 3CO becomes 3CO2.\nThe balanced equation is Fe2O3 + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO2.\nThe sum of coefficients is 1 + 3 + 2 + 3 = 9.\nCO gains oxygen to become CO2, so CO is oxidised. Fe2O3 loses oxygen, so it is reduced.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-059","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":59,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (coefficient asked in the balanced equation) with List-II (value of that coefficient).\nList-I\n(a) NH$_3$ in N$_2$ + H$_2$ → NH$_3$\n(b) BaSO$_4$ in BaCl$_2$ + Al$_2$(SO$_4$)$_3$ → BaSO$_4$ + AlCl$_3$\n(c) Al$_2$(SO$_4$)$_3$ in the same reaction as (b)\n(d) H$_2$ in Fe + H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + H$_2$\nList-II\n(i) 1  (ii) 2  (iii) 3  (iv) 4","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The balanced equation is N$_2$ + 3H$_2$ → 2NH$_3$, so the coefficient of NH$_3$ is 2, giving (ii).\n(b) The balanced equation is 3BaCl$_2$ + Al$_2$(SO$_4$)$_3$ → 3BaSO$_4$ + 2AlCl$_3$, so the coefficient of BaSO$_4$ is 3, giving (iii).\n(c) In the same equation, the coefficient of Al$_2$(SO$_4$)$_3$ is 1, giving (i).\n(d) The balanced equation is 3Fe + 4H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + 4H$_2$, so the coefficient of H$_2$ is 4, giving (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-060","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":60,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is a combination reaction?","image":"","options":["$CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$","$H_2 + Cl_2 \\rightarrow 2HCl$","$Zn + CuSO_4 \\rightarrow ZnSO_4 + Cu$","$Na_2SO_4 + BaCl_2 \\rightarrow BaSO_4 + 2NaCl$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A combination reaction forms a single product from two or more reactants.\n$H_2 + Cl_2 \\rightarrow 2HCl$: two elements give only one product, HCl, so it is a combination reaction.\nThe heating of $CaCO_3$ is a decomposition, because one reactant gives two products.\nThe zinc reaction is a displacement, and the last reaction is a double displacement. Both of these give two products.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-061","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":61,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Iron railing left in humid air\n(b) Silver ornament left exposed for months\n(c) Copper vessel left exposed for a long time\n(d) Fried snacks kept in an open container for weeks\nList-II\n(i) Black coating on the surface\n(ii) Green coating on the surface\n(iii) Reddish-brown powdery coating\n(iv) Change in smell and taste due to oxidation\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Rusting of iron gives a reddish-brown powdery coating, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Corrosion of silver gives a black coating, so (b)-(i).\n(c) Corrosion of copper gives a green coating, so (c)-(ii).\n(d) Fats and oils in food get oxidised and become rancid, so their smell and taste change. This gives (d)-(iv).\nOption (2) is the tempting wrong choice because it swaps the colours for silver and copper.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-062","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":62,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match the reaction in List-I with the element that is displaced (set free) in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) $Fe + CuSO_4$\n(b) $Mg + $ dilute $HCl$\n(c) $Cu + AgNO_3$\n(d) $Fe_2O_3 + Al$\nList-II\n(i) Silver\n(ii) Iron\n(iii) Copper\n(iv) Hydrogen","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Iron displaces copper from copper sulphate, so copper is set free: (iii).\n(b) Magnesium displaces hydrogen from HCl, and $H_2$ gas is released: (iv).\n(c) Copper displaces silver from silver nitrate, as in silver refining: (i).\n(d) Aluminium displaces iron from iron oxide: (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-063","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":63,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes are exothermic?\n(i) Burning of natural gas\n(ii) Decomposition of vegetable matter into compost\n(iii) Decomposition of silver chloride in sunlight\n(iv) Respiration\n(v) Electrolysis of water\n(vi) Reaction of calcium oxide with water","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Exothermic: burning of methane gives out heat.\n(ii) Exothermic: composting releases heat.\n(iii) Endothermic: light energy is absorbed.\n(iv) Exothermic: energy is released.\n(v) Endothermic: electrical energy is absorbed.\n(vi) Exothermic: a large amount of heat is released.\nSo 4 processes are exothermic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-064","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":64,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Magnesium ribbon burns in air with a dazzling white flame to form a white powder. Which of the following is the correctly balanced equation for this combination reaction?","image":"","options":["$Mg + O_2 \\rightarrow MgO$","$Mg + O_2 \\rightarrow MgO_2$","$2Mg + O_2 \\rightarrow 2MgO$","$Mg + 2O \\rightarrow MgO_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The white powder is magnesium oxide, whose formula is MgO.\nIn the skeletal equation $Mg + O_2 \\rightarrow MgO$, oxygen is unbalanced: 2 atoms on the left, 1 on the right.\nWe may change only coefficients, never formulae. Writing $2MgO$ balances oxygen.\nThen $2Mg$ balances magnesium: $2Mg + O_2 \\rightarrow 2MgO$.\nOptions 2 and 4 wrongly change the formula of the product to $MgO_2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-065","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":65,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the equation $BaCl_2(aq) + Na_2SO_4(aq) \\rightarrow BaSO_4(s) + 2NaCl(aq)$, barium sulphate is given the symbol (s) although the reaction occurs in solution.\nReason (R): Barium sulphate is insoluble in water and separates out as a white precipitate.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the equation shows $BaSO_4$ as (s), while the other substances are (aq).\nR is true: mixing the two solutions gives an insoluble white precipitate of $BaSO_4$.\nThe product is a solid because it is insoluble, so R correctly explains A.\nNaCl stays dissolved, which is why it is written (aq).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-066","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":66,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the reaction:\n$Fe_2O_3 + 2Al \\rightarrow Al_2O_3 + 2Fe$\nThis reaction is an example of a","image":"","options":["combination reaction","decomposition reaction","displacement reaction","double displacement reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In this reaction, aluminium, a free element, takes the place of iron in iron oxide, and iron is set free.\nWhen one element removes another element from its compound, the reaction is a displacement reaction.\nIt is not double displacement, because no two compounds exchange ions.\nIt is not combination, because two products form. It is not decomposition, because there are two reactants.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-067","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":67,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Manufacturers flush bags of potato chips with nitrogen gas before sealing them.\nReason (R): Nitrogen keeps oxygen away from the fats and oils in the chips, which slows down their oxidation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that chips bags are flushed with nitrogen.\nR is true: nitrogen replaces air, so less oxygen can reach the fats and oils.\nOxidised fats and oils become rancid, and their smell and taste change. Preventing oxidation prevents rancidity, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-068","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":68,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"While balancing Fe + H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + H$_2$, a student must make the four oxygen atoms on the right equal to the oxygen atoms on the left. Which way of writing water is correct?","image":"","options":["H$_2$O$_4$","(H$_2$O)$_4$","4H$_2$O","H$_8$O$_4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rule: while balancing, the formula inside each 'box' must never be changed. Only a coefficient may be placed in front of it.\nH$_2$O$_4$ and H$_8$O$_4$ change the formula, so they no longer represent water.\n(H$_2$O)$_4$ is not the accepted way to show four molecules.\n4H$_2$O correctly shows four water molecules, which contain 4 O atoms.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-069","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":69,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Use only the displacement reactions described in NCERT: iron, zinc and lead displace copper from its salts, and copper displaces silver from silver nitrate.\nHow many of the following combinations will show a displacement reaction?\n(i) Zinc + copper sulphate solution\n(ii) Copper + iron sulphate solution\n(iii) Lead + copper chloride solution\n(iv) Silver + copper sulphate solution\n(v) Zinc + silver nitrate solution","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: zinc is more reactive than copper.\n(ii) No: iron displaces copper, so copper is less reactive than iron and cannot displace it.\n(iii) Yes: lead displaces copper from copper chloride.\n(iv) No: copper displaces silver, so silver is less reactive than copper.\n(v) Yes: zinc is more reactive than copper, which in turn is more reactive than silver. So $Zn + 2AgNO_3 \\rightarrow Zn(NO_3)_2 + 2Ag$ occurs.\nThree combinations react.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-070","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":70,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In Activity 1.10, sodium sulphate solution is mixed with barium chloride solution and a white substance that is insoluble in water is formed. Which pair of ions combines to form this white substance?","image":"","options":["$Ba^{2+}$ and $SO_4^{2-}$","$Na^{+}$ and $Cl^{-}$","$Na^{+}$ and $SO_4^{2-}$","$Ba^{2+}$ and $Cl^{-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The reaction is $Na_2SO_4(aq) + BaCl_2(aq) \\rightarrow BaSO_4(s) + 2NaCl(aq)$.\nThe reactants exchange ions, so $Ba^{2+}$ pairs with $SO_4^{2-}$ to give insoluble barium sulphate. This is the white precipitate.\n$Na^{+}$ and $Cl^{-}$ form sodium chloride, which stays dissolved in the solution, so option (2) is wrong.\nOptions (3) and (4) are the original reactant pairings, not the new product.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-071","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":71,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student cleans a 3 cm magnesium ribbon with sandpaper and burns it in air using tongs. Which observation correctly describes this activity?","image":"","options":["It burns with a blue flame and leaves a black residue of magnesium oxide.","It burns with a dazzling white flame and changes into a white powder of magnesium oxide.","It burns with a yellow flame and forms a yellow powder of magnesium nitrate.","It melts without burning and forms grey drops of magnesium."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When magnesium is heated in air, it reacts with oxygen.\nIt burns with a dazzling white flame.\nThe product is a white powder, magnesium oxide (MgO), which is collected in a watch-glass.\nA black residue or a yellow nitrate does not form, and the ribbon does not simply melt.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-072","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":72,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following best describes a precipitate?","image":"","options":["A gas given off during a chemical reaction","An insoluble solid formed when solutions react","A reactant that remains unused after a reaction","A solid that dissolves completely in water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines a precipitate as the insoluble substance formed in a reaction, for example $BaSO_4$ in Activity 1.10.\nAny reaction that produces a precipitate is called a precipitation reaction.\nA gas is not a precipitate, and an unused reactant is not a product at all.\nA solid that dissolves completely is soluble, which is the opposite of a precipitate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-073","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":73,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Before burning a magnesium ribbon in air, it is rubbed with sandpaper. If this step is skipped, the ribbon does not catch fire easily. What is the best reason for this?","image":"","options":["Sandpaper adds a thin layer of oxygen to the ribbon, which helps it burn","The ribbon is covered with a layer of magnesium oxide that stops the metal underneath from reacting with oxygen","Rubbing heats the ribbon to its ignition temperature","Sandpaper removes moisture, and moisture makes magnesium oxide decompose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"On standing in air, magnesium slowly reacts with oxygen and forms a thin coating of magnesium oxide on its surface.\nThis coating keeps oxygen away from the fresh metal underneath, so the ribbon does not burn easily.\nRubbing with sandpaper removes the layer and exposes clean magnesium.\nThe clean metal then burns with a dazzling white flame and forms white MgO powder.\nRubbing produces very little heat, so it cannot raise the ribbon to its ignition temperature.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-074","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":74,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about rusting of iron and rancidity of fried snacks:\n(i) Both involve oxidation by oxygen from the air.\n(ii) Both can be slowed down by reducing contact with air.\n(iii) Both are decomposition reactions in which a single substance breaks down using light energy.\n(iv) Both produce a reddish-brown coating on the substance.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (iii) only","(ii) and (iv) only","(i) and (ii) only","(i), (ii) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: rusting is the oxidation of iron, and rancidity is the oxidation of fats and oils.\n(ii) Correct: painting iron, or sealing food in airtight containers or nitrogen-filled packets, keeps oxygen away and slows both processes.\n(iii) Incorrect: both are oxidation processes, not decomposition reactions caused by light.\n(iv) Incorrect: only rust is reddish brown. Rancidity changes the smell and taste of food.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-075","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":75,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the balanced equation $3Fe(s) + 4H_2O(g) \\rightarrow Fe_3O_4(s) + 4H_2(g)$, the symbol (g) written with $H_2O$ tells us that","image":"","options":["water is present as a solution","water is used in the form of steam","water is produced as a gas during the reaction","water acts as a catalyst"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The symbol (g) means the gaseous state.\nWater in the gaseous state is steam, so iron reacts with steam in this reaction.\n$H_2O$ is on the reactant side, so it is not produced. This rules out option (3).\nIt is consumed (4 molecules are used), so it is not a catalyst.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-076","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":76,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Respiration is an endothermic process.\nReason (R): In respiration, glucose combines with oxygen in the cells of the body.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false: respiration releases energy. NCERT calls it an exothermic process.\nThe equation is C6H12O6 + 6O2 → 6CO2 + 6H2O + energy.\nR is true: glucose obtained from digested carbohydrates combines with oxygen in the cells.\nIt is this energy-releasing combination that makes respiration exothermic.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-077","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":77,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the electrolysis of water set-up (water with a few drops of dilute sulphuric acid, carbon electrodes, 6 V battery), gas collects in two inverted test tubes A and B. The volume of gas in test tube A is about double that in test tube B. When a burning candle is brought near the mouth of test tube A, what is observed, and why?","image":"","options":["The gas burns with a pop sound because it is hydrogen","The candle burns more brightly because the gas is oxygen","The candle is put out because the gas is carbon dioxide","The gas burns with a pop sound because it is oxygen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Water decomposes as 2H2O → 2H2 + O2, so two volumes of hydrogen form for every one volume of oxygen.\nThe tube with double the volume (A) therefore holds hydrogen.\nHydrogen burns with a pop sound when a flame is brought near it.\nOxygen would make the candle burn brighter, but oxygen is the smaller volume, in tube B.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-078","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":78,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the balanced reaction 3Fe + 4H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + 4H$_2$, in which iron is heated in steam. Which of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Iron is oxidised.\n(ii) Steam is reduced.\n(iii) The reaction is a redox reaction.\n(iv) Steam is oxidised because hydrogen gas is released from it.","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(iii) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Fe gains oxygen and forms Fe$_3$O$_4$.\n(ii) Correct: H$_2$O loses its oxygen and becomes H$_2$, so steam is reduced.\n(iii) Correct: one reactant is oxidised and the other reduced, so this is a redox reaction.\n(iv) Incorrect: water gives up oxygen, not just hydrogen, and losing oxygen is reduction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-079","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":79,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Quick lime reacts vigorously with water to form slaked lime. What is the chemical formula of slaked lime?","image":"","options":["$CaO$","$CaCO_3$","$Ca(OH)_2$","$CaCl_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Quick lime is calcium oxide, $CaO$.\nIt combines with water: $CaO + H_2O \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2$.\nThe product, calcium hydroxide $Ca(OH)_2$, is called slaked lime.\n$CaO$ is the reactant (quick lime), not the product.\n$CaCO_3$ is limestone or marble.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-080","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":80,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a decomposition reaction?","image":"","options":["$CaO + H_2O \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2$","$ZnCO_3 \\rightarrow ZnO + CO_2$","$Fe + CuSO_4 \\rightarrow FeSO_4 + Cu$","$Na_2SO_4 + BaCl_2 \\rightarrow BaSO_4 + 2NaCl$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a decomposition reaction, a single reactant breaks down into two or more simpler products.\n$ZnCO_3$ alone breaks into $ZnO$ and $CO_2$ on heating, so it is a thermal decomposition.\n(1) is a combination reaction: two reactants give one product.\n(3) is a displacement reaction.\n(4) is a double displacement (precipitation) reaction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-081","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":81,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the balanced equation 2HNO$_3$ + Ca(OH)$_2$ → Ca(NO$_3$)$_2$ + 2H$_2$O, the total mass of the reactants equals the total mass of the products.\nReason (R): In this balanced equation, the number of molecules on the reactant side equals the number of molecules on the product side.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: atoms balance as H 4 = 4, N 2 = 2, O 8 = 8 and Ca 1 = 1, so mass is conserved.\nR is true here: there are 2 + 1 = 3 molecules on the left and 1 + 2 = 3 on the right.\nHowever, mass is conserved because the atoms of each element are equal on both sides, not because molecule counts match.\nFor example, in 3Fe + 4H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + 4H$_2$, the counts differ (7 vs 5) yet mass is conserved.\nSo R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-082","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":82,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are exothermic processes?\n(i) Burning of natural gas\n(ii) Respiration\n(iii) Decomposition of vegetable matter into compost\n(iv) Adding water to quick lime\n(v) Heating limestone to form quick lime\n(vi) Silver chloride turning grey in sunlight\n(vii) Mixing barium hydroxide with ammonium chloride","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Exothermic: burning releases heat.\n(ii) Exothermic: glucose is oxidised to release energy.\n(iii) Exothermic: NCERT lists composting as exothermic.\n(iv) Exothermic: CaO + H2O releases a large amount of heat.\n(v) Endothermic: thermal decomposition needs heat.\n(vi) Endothermic: the decomposition absorbs light energy.\n(vii) Endothermic: the test tube feels cold.\nThe count of exothermic processes is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-083","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":83,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Iron railing in humid air\n(B) Silver ornaments left exposed\n(C) Copper statue in open air\n(D) Cooking oil stored for long in an open bottle\nList-II\n(i) Black coating\n(ii) Change in smell and taste\n(iii) Reddish-brown coating\n(iv) Green coating\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: Iron rusts and gets a reddish-brown coating, so A matches (iii).\nB: Silver tarnishes with a black coating, so B matches (i).\nC: Copper develops a green coating, so C matches (iv).\nD: Oxidised oil turns rancid, and its smell and taste change, so D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-084","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":84,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the reaction $CuO + H_2 \\rightarrow Cu + H_2O$, hydrogen is oxidised.\nReason (R): A substance that gains oxygen during a reaction is said to be oxidised.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Hydrogen combines with the oxygen removed from CuO and forms $H_2O$.\nR is true. NCERT defines oxidation as the gain of oxygen (or the loss of hydrogen).\nHydrogen is oxidised precisely because it gains oxygen, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-085","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":85,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the reaction MnO$_2$ + 4HCl → MnCl$_2$ + 2H$_2$O + Cl$_2$, HCl is oxidised.\nReason (R): HCl loses hydrogen when it is converted into Cl$_2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that HCl is oxidised to Cl$_2$ in this reaction.\nR is true: HCl contains hydrogen, while Cl$_2$ contains none, so hydrogen has been removed.\nLoss of hydrogen is oxidation, so R correctly explains A.\nMnO$_2$ loses oxygen and is reduced to MnCl$_2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-086","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":86,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student sets up Activity 1.7: two carbon electrodes are fixed through the base of a plastic mug, connected to a 6 V battery, and the mug is filled with water containing a few drops of dilute sulphuric acid. Test tubes filled with water are inverted over the electrodes and the current is switched on. Which of the following observations is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Bubbles of gas form at both electrodes.","The gases formed push water out of the inverted test tubes.","Equal volumes of gas collect in the two test tubes.","The reaction is a decomposition brought about by electrical energy."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: gas bubbles appear at both electrodes.\n(2) Correct: the gases displace water from the inverted tubes.\n(3) Incorrect: the volumes are unequal. Hydrogen collects in double the volume of oxygen.\n(4) Correct: electricity breaks water into hydrogen and oxygen, which is an electrolytic decomposition.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-087","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":87,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly matches a metal with the colour of the coating formed on it by corrosion?","image":"","options":["Silver – black coating","Copper – reddish brown coating","Iron – green coating","Silver – green coating"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT gives these corrosion coatings:\n- Iron rusts to form a reddish brown coating.\n- Copper develops a green coating.\n- Silver develops a black coating.\nOptions 2, 3 and 4 swap these colours between the metals. Only silver – black is correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-088","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":88,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In Activity 1.11, about 1 g of copper powder is heated in a china dish. What is observed on the surface of the powder?","image":"","options":["It becomes coated with a green layer","It becomes coated with a black layer","It becomes coated with a reddish brown layer","It turns into a white powder"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Heating copper in air makes it combine with oxygen: 2Cu + O$_2$ → 2CuO.\nCopper(II) oxide is black, so the brown copper powder gets a black coating.\nA green coating forms when copper corrodes slowly in moist air, not when it is heated.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-089","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":89,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Read the case and answer.\nA white solid X, which has the same formula as marble, is heated strongly. It gives a white solid Y and a gas Z. Y reacts vigorously with water to form W, and a solution of W is used to whitewash walls. On the wall, W reacts with Z from the air and forms X again.\nWhich statement about this case is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["X is calcium carbonate, CaCO3","The conversion of X into Y and Z absorbs energy","The reaction of Y with water is a combination reaction that releases a large amount of heat","The shiny layer of X forms immediately after W is applied, because W reacts very rapidly with Z"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The substances are X = CaCO3, Y = CaO, Z = CO2 and W = Ca(OH)2.\n(1) Correct: marble is also CaCO3.\n(2) Correct: thermal decomposition needs heat, so it absorbs energy.\n(3) Correct: CaO + H2O → Ca(OH)2 + heat is a combination reaction.\n(4) Incorrect: Ca(OH)2 reacts slowly with CO2, and the shiny CaCO3 layer forms only after two to three days.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-090","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":90,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the hit-and-trial method for balancing Fe + H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + H$_2$, which compound and which element in it are chosen first?","image":"","options":["H$_2$O and hydrogen","Fe$_3$O$_4$ and oxygen","Fe$_3$O$_4$ and iron","H$_2$ and hydrogen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Method: start with the compound that has the most atoms. Here that is Fe$_3$O$_4$, with 7 atoms.\nWithin that compound, pick the element with the most atoms. Oxygen has 4, iron has 3.\nSo Fe$_3$O$_4$ and oxygen are chosen first.\nOption (3) is tempting, but iron (3 atoms) has fewer atoms than oxygen (4 atoms).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-091","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":91,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (everyday observation) with List-II (chemical explanation).\nList-I:\n(A) Green coating on an old copper vessel\n(B) Packets of chips flushed with nitrogen\n(C) Whitewashed walls develop a shiny finish after 2–3 days\n(D) Use of silver bromide in black and white photography\nList-II:\n(i) Decomposition caused by light\n(ii) Formation of calcium carbonate\n(iii) Corrosion\n(iv) Prevention of rancidity","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The green coating forms when copper is attacked by substances in its surroundings. This is corrosion: (iii).\n(B) Nitrogen keeps the fats in chips from being oxidised. This prevents rancidity: (iv).\n(C) Ca(OH)2 reacts with CO2 in air to form a thin, shiny layer of CaCO3: (ii).\n(D) 2AgBr → 2Ag + Br2 takes place in sunlight. This is decomposition by light: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-092","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":92,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"When lead nitrate powder is heated strongly in a boiling tube, brown fumes are seen. These fumes are of:","image":"","options":["Oxygen","Nitrogen dioxide","Lead oxide vapour","Nitrogen monoxide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"On heating, lead nitrate decomposes as $2Pb(NO_3)_2(s) \\rightarrow 2PbO(s) + 4NO_2(g) + O_2(g)$.\nNCERT states that the brown fumes are of nitrogen dioxide ($NO_2$).\nOxygen is also released, but it is colourless, so it cannot give brown fumes.\nLead oxide is left behind as a solid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-093","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":93,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In a group activity, two spatulas of ammonium nitrate are added to 25 mL of water in beaker B, and two spatulas of anhydrous copper sulphate are added to 25 mL of water in beaker C. The temperatures are recorded before and after stirring.\nStatement I: The temperature in beaker B falls.\nStatement II: The temperature in beaker C rises.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Dissolving ammonium nitrate in water absorbs heat, so the water cools. This is an endothermic change.\nStatement II is correct. Anhydrous copper sulphate takes up water and releases heat, so the temperature rises. This is an exothermic change.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-094","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":94,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Chemical equations must always be balanced. Which statement best explains why?","image":"","options":["Atoms of one element change into atoms of another element during a reaction","Mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction, so the number of atoms of each element must stay the same","The number of molecules on both sides of a reaction must always be equal","Balancing tells us the physical states of the reactants and products"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: the law of conservation of mass says mass is neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.\nSo the number of atoms of each element must be the same before and after the reaction.\nOption (1) is wrong because atoms never change into other elements.\nOption (3) is wrong because only atoms are conserved, not molecules.\nOption (4) is wrong because physical states are shown by the symbols (s), (l), (g) and (aq), not by balancing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-095","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":95,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about combination reactions is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$H_2 + Cl_2 \\rightarrow 2HCl$ is a combination reaction because two elements form a single compound.","In $2H_2 + O_2 \\rightarrow 2H_2O$, the number of molecules changes from 3 to 2, but the number of atoms of each element stays the same.","In every balanced combination reaction, the total number of molecules on the reactant side must equal that on the product side.","$CaO + H_2O \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2$ shows that compounds, not only elements, can take part in combination reactions."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Option 1 is correct: two elements give one product, HCl.\nOption 2 is correct: there are 3 molecules on the left and 2 on the right. Each side still has 4 H atoms and 2 O atoms.\nOption 3 is incorrect: balancing conserves atoms, not molecules. The water-forming reaction above disproves this claim.\nOption 4 is correct: both CaO and $H_2O$ are compounds, yet they combine to form a single product.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-096","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":96,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Copper powder is heated in air until it turns black. Hydrogen gas is then passed over the heated black material, and it turns brown again. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In the first step, copper is oxidised.\n(ii) In the second step, the colour changes from black to brown because copper(II) oxide is reduced to copper.\n(iii) In the second step, hydrogen is the substance that gets reduced.\n(iv) In $2PbO + C \\rightarrow 2Pb + CO_2$, lead oxide is reduced because it loses oxygen, just as CuO does in the second step.\n(v) In $ZnO + C \\rightarrow Zn + CO$, carbon is reduced because it combines with oxygen.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: copper gains oxygen and forms CuO.\n(ii) Correct: CuO loses oxygen and changes back to brown copper.\n(iii) Incorrect: hydrogen gains oxygen and forms $H_2O$, so it is oxidised, not reduced.\n(iv) Correct: PbO loses oxygen and becomes Pb, so it is reduced.\n(v) Incorrect: combining with oxygen is oxidation, so carbon is oxidised.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-097","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":97,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The equation $Mg + O_2 \\rightarrow MgO$ is a skeletal chemical equation.\nReason (R): The number of oxygen atoms is not the same on the two sides of this equation, so mass is not equal on both sides.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Count the atoms on each side.\nLHS: 1 Mg and 2 O. RHS: 1 Mg and 1 O.\nOxygen is not balanced, so the total mass on the two sides is not equal.\nAn equation written with formulae but not balanced is called a skeletal equation, so A is true.\nR is true and gives exactly the reason the equation is skeletal.\nThe balanced form is $2Mg + O_2 \\rightarrow 2MgO$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-098","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":98,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In a closed flask, 6.5 g of zinc reacts completely with 9.8 g of sulphuric acid according to Zn + H$_2$SO$_4$ → ZnSO$_4$ + H$_2$. The reaction forms 16.1 g of zinc sulphate. What mass of hydrogen gas is produced?","image":"","options":["0.1 g","0.2 g","2.0 g","3.3 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Zn = 6.5 g, H$_2$SO$_4$ = 9.8 g, ZnSO$_4$ = 16.1 g.\nFormula: by the law of conservation of mass, mass of reactants = mass of products.\nSubstituting: 6.5 + 9.8 = 16.1 + mass of H$_2$, which gives mass of H$_2$ = 16.3 − 16.1.\nSo the mass of hydrogen = 0.2 g.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-099","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":99,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Butter kept open for many days starts smelling and tasting unpleasant. This change is called:","image":"","options":["Corrosion","Rancidity","Fermentation","Precipitation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When fats and oils are oxidised, their smell and taste change. This is called rancidity.\nButter is a fat, so the change in butter left open is rancidity.\nCorrosion applies to metals, and precipitation is the formation of an insoluble solid in a solution.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-100","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":100,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"White silver chloride kept in a china dish in sunlight slowly turns grey. The grey colour is due to the formation of:","image":"","options":["chlorine gas","silver oxide","silver metal","silver nitrate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sunlight decomposes silver chloride: $2AgCl(s) \\rightarrow 2Ag(s) + Cl_2(g)$.\nThe silver metal formed gives the solid its grey colour.\nChlorine is a gas that escapes, so it cannot colour the solid. Oxygen and nitrate are not involved in this reaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-101","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":101,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"To balance oxygen atoms in Fe + H2O → Fe3O4 + H2, four oxygen atoms are needed on the left side. Which way of writing this is correct?","image":"","options":["H$_2$O$_4$","(H$_2$O)$_4$","H$_8$O$_4$","4H$_2$O"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"While balancing, a formula is kept 'inside a box' and must not be changed.\nWriting H$_2$O$_4$ or H$_8$O$_4$ changes the formula, so these describe a different substance, not water.\nWriting (H$_2$O)$_4$ is also not allowed.\nOnly a coefficient may be placed in front of the formula, so 4H$_2$O is correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-102","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":102,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Iron nails are kept in copper sulphate solution for about 20 minutes.\nStatement I: The blue colour of the copper sulphate solution fades, and the iron nails become brownish in colour.\nStatement II: Zinc and lead can also displace copper from its compounds because they are more reactive than copper.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In $Fe + CuSO_4 \\rightarrow FeSO_4 + Cu$, copper is deposited on the nail (brownish coat). The blue $CuSO_4$ is used up, so its colour fades.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT gives $Zn + CuSO_4$ and $Pb + CuCl_2$ as displacement reactions, because Zn and Pb are more reactive than Cu.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-103","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":103,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the equation 3Fe(s) + 4H2O(g) → Fe3O4(s) + 4H2(g), the symbol (g) with water shows that the water is used as steam.\nStatement II: The symbol (aq) is written with a reactant or product that is present as a solution in water.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: (g) means the gaseous state. Iron reacts with water in the form of steam here.\nStatement II is correct: 'aqueous' (aq) means the substance is dissolved in water.\nThe other state symbols are (s) for solid and (l) for liquid.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-104","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":104,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following best describes corrosion?","image":"","options":["Melting of a metal on strong heating","Attack on a metal by substances around it, such as moisture and acids","Conversion of a metal into an alloy","Loss of shine of a metal due to rubbing"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines corrosion as the process in which a metal is attacked by substances around it, such as moisture and acids.\nMelting and loss of shine by rubbing are physical changes. No new substance is formed in either.\nForming an alloy is the deliberate mixing of metals, not an attack on a metal by its surroundings.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-105","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":105,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Case: A student heats about 2 g of green ferrous sulphate crystals in a dry boiling tube. The colour of the crystals changes, and a smell is noticed.\nConsider the statements:\n(i) The crystals first lose their water of crystallisation, which changes their colour.\n(ii) The decomposition gives one solid product and two gaseous products.\n(iii) This is a thermal decomposition reaction.\n(iv) The characteristic smell is due to ammonia gas released.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $FeSO_4\\cdot 7H_2O$ loses water on heating, and its colour changes.\n(ii) Correct: $2FeSO_4 \\rightarrow Fe_2O_3(s) + SO_2(g) + SO_3(g)$ gives one solid and two gases.\n(iii) Correct: the decomposition is brought about by heat.\n(iv) Incorrect: the smell is that of burning sulphur (sulphur oxides). No nitrogen is present, so ammonia cannot form.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-106","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":106,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student keeps white silver chloride in a china dish in sunlight. After some time it turns grey. The grey colour is due to the formation of:","image":"","options":["Silver oxide","Chlorine gas","Silver metal","Silver nitrate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Sunlight decomposes silver chloride: $2AgCl(s) \\xrightarrow{Sunlight} 2Ag(s) + Cl_2(g)$.\nThe grey colour comes from finely divided silver metal.\nChlorine is also formed, but it is a gas that escapes, so it does not cause the grey colour.\nNo oxygen or nitrate is involved, so silver oxide and silver nitrate cannot form.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-107","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":107,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Read the two statements.\nStatement I: In the reaction $MnO_2 + 4HCl \\to MnCl_2 + 2H_2O + Cl_2$, HCl is oxidised because it loses hydrogen.\nStatement II: In the reaction $ZnO + C \\to Zn + CO$, zinc oxide is oxidised because carbon combines with its oxygen.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. HCl loses hydrogen and becomes $Cl_2$. Loss of hydrogen is oxidation.\nStatement II is incorrect. ZnO loses oxygen and becomes Zn, so ZnO is reduced.\nIt is carbon that gains oxygen to form CO, so carbon is the substance oxidised.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-108","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":108,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): White silver chloride kept in a china dish in sunlight slowly turns grey.\nReason (R): Light decomposes silver chloride into silver metal and chlorine gas.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: white AgCl turns grey when left in sunlight.\nR is true: 2AgCl(s) → 2Ag(s) + Cl2(g) in the presence of sunlight.\nThe grey colour comes from the silver metal formed, so R correctly explains A.\nThis light-driven decomposition is used in black and white photography.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-109","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":109,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In Activity 1.7 (electrolysis of water using carbon electrodes and a 6 V battery), a few drops of dilute sulphuric acid are added to the water. What is the main purpose of adding the acid?","image":"","options":["To supply extra hydrogen gas for collection","To make the water conduct electricity better","To stop the carbon electrodes from corroding","To absorb the oxygen formed at one electrode"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pure water conducts electricity very poorly, so very little current flows through it.\nA few drops of dilute sulphuric acid increase the conductivity of the water, so the current can decompose it.\nThe acid is not the source of the gases collected. Both hydrogen and oxygen come from water.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-110","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":110,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match each substance in List-I with the state symbol used for it in the NCERT equations in List-II.\nList-I\nP. $H_2O$ in the reaction of iron with steam\nQ. $CH_3OH$ formed from CO and $H_2$ at 340 atm\nR. Glucose formed in photosynthesis\nS. $Fe_3O_4$ formed when iron reacts with steam\nList-II\n(i) (l)\n(ii) (g)\n(iii) (aq)\n(iv) (s)","image":"","options":["P-(ii), Q-(i), R-(iii), S-(iv)","P-(i), Q-(ii), R-(iv), S-(iii)","P-(ii), Q-(iii), R-(i), S-(iv)","P-(iii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P: Steam is gaseous water, so $H_2O(g)$. This is (ii).\nQ: Methanol is obtained as a liquid, so $CH_3OH(l)$. This is (i).\nR: In the photosynthesis equation, glucose is written $C_6H_{12}O_6(aq)$. This is (iii).\nS: Iron oxide is a solid, so $Fe_3O_4(s)$. This is (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-111","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":111,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (type of reaction).\nList-I:\n(A) CaO + H2O → Ca(OH)2\n(B) CaCO3 → CaO + CO2 (on heating)\n(C) Fe + CuSO4 → FeSO4 + Cu\n(D) Na2SO4 + BaCl2 → BaSO4 + 2NaCl\nList-II:\n(i) Double displacement\n(ii) Combination\n(iii) Displacement\n(iv) Decomposition","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) Two reactants form a single product, so it is a combination reaction: (ii).\n(B) A single reactant breaks into simpler products on heating, so it is decomposition: (iv).\n(C) Iron displaces copper from its salt, so it is displacement: (iii).\n(D) The reactants exchange ions and give a BaSO4 precipitate, so it is double displacement: (i).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-112","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":112,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (type of reaction).\nList-I:\n(P) 2AgBr → 2Ag + Br2 (in sunlight)\n(Q) Pb + CuCl2 → PbCl2 + Cu\n(R) Na2SO4 + BaCl2 → BaSO4 + 2NaCl\n(S) C + O2 → CO2\nList-II:\n(1) Displacement\n(2) Combination\n(3) Decomposition\n(4) Double displacement","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3","P-1, Q-3, R-4, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P: One reactant breaks down using light energy, so it is decomposition (3).\nQ: The more reactive lead displaces copper from its compound, so it is displacement (1).\nR: The two compounds exchange ions and form a BaSO4 precipitate, so it is double displacement (4).\nS: Two reactants form a single product, so it is combination (2).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-113","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":113,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"About 2 g of barium hydroxide is mixed with 1 g of ammonium chloride in a test tube, and the bottom of the tube is touched with the palm. What is felt, and why?","image":"","options":["The tube feels warm because heat is released by the reaction","The tube feels cold because the reaction absorbs heat from the surroundings","The tube feels warm because the reaction absorbs heat","The tube feels cold because heat is released into the mixture"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The reaction between $Ba(OH)_2$ and $NH_4Cl$ absorbs heat from its surroundings, including the test tube and your palm.\nThe tube therefore feels cold, which marks the reaction as endothermic.\nOptions (3) and (4) mix up the observation and the reason: absorbing heat makes the tube cold, and releasing heat would make it warm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-114","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":114,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The decomposition of silver bromide, $2AgBr(s) \\rightarrow 2Ag(s) + Br_2(g)$, used in black-and-white photography, is brought about by:","image":"","options":["heat energy","light energy","electrical energy","mechanical pressure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT shows the decomposition of silver bromide taking place in sunlight, just like silver chloride.\nSo light provides the energy for this decomposition. This is why the reaction is useful in black-and-white photography.\nHeat drives thermal decomposition, such as that of $CaCO_3$. Electricity drives electrolysis, such as that of water.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-115","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":115,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Dilute hydrochloric acid is added to iron filings in a test tube. What are the products formed?","image":"","options":["Chlorine gas and iron hydroxide","Iron chloride and hydrogen gas","Iron oxide and water","No reaction takes place"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Iron is a reactive metal and displaces hydrogen from dilute HCl.\nThe products are iron chloride and hydrogen gas, which is seen as bubbles.\nMagnesium reacts the same way: $Mg + 2HCl \\rightarrow MgCl_2 + H_2$.\nChlorine is not released, because the metal takes up the chloride part and hydrogen is set free.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-116","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":116,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match the observation in List-I with the reaction responsible for it in List-II.\nList-I (Observation)\nP. Brown fumes come out of a heated boiling tube\nQ. Green crystals change colour on heating and a smell of burning sulphur is noticed\nR. The blue colour of a solution fades and a brownish coating forms on the metal dipped in it\nS. A white insoluble substance forms on mixing two colourless solutions\nList-II (Reaction)\n(i) $Fe + CuSO_4 \\to FeSO_4 + Cu$\n(ii) $2Pb(NO_3)_2 \\to 2PbO + 4NO_2 + O_2$\n(iii) $Na_2SO_4 + BaCl_2 \\to BaSO_4 + 2NaCl$\n(iv) $2FeSO_4 \\to Fe_2O_3 + SO_2 + SO_3$","image":"","options":["P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(iii)","P-(iv), Q-(ii), R-(i), S-(iii)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(iii), S-(i)","P-(iv), Q-(ii), R-(iii), S-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P: The brown fumes are $NO_2$, released when lead nitrate is heated, so P matches (ii).\nQ: Green ferrous sulphate crystals lose water and decompose, giving off $SO_2$ and $SO_3$ (burning sulphur smell), so Q matches (iv).\nR: Iron displaces copper, so the blue $CuSO_4$ fades and copper coats the nail, so R matches (i).\nS: Mixing the two solutions gives a white precipitate of $BaSO_4$, so S matches (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-117","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":117,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Walls whitewashed with a solution of slaked lime develop a shiny finish two to three days after whitewashing. Which substance is responsible for this shine?","image":"","options":["Calcium oxide formed by drying","Calcium carbonate formed by reaction with carbon dioxide of air","Calcium hydroxide crystals left after water evaporates","Calcium sulphate formed by reaction with moisture"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Calcium hydroxide on the wall slowly reacts with $CO_2$ in the air: $Ca(OH)_2 + CO_2 \\rightarrow CaCO_3 + H_2O$.\nThis forms a thin layer of calcium carbonate, which gives the shiny finish.\nThe change is slow, so the shine appears only after two to three days.\nPlain dried $Ca(OH)_2$ (option 3) does not give this finish.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-118","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":118,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Physical states of reactants and products are always included in every chemical equation.\nStatement II: Mentioning physical states along with chemical formulae makes a chemical equation more informative.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. NCERT says physical states are usually not included unless it is necessary to specify them.\nStatement II is true. State symbols (s), (l), (g) and (aq) add information about each substance's state.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-119","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":119,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the electrolysis of water, the volume of hydrogen collected is double the volume of oxygen collected.\nReason (R): Water contains hydrogen and oxygen in the ratio 2 : 1 by mass.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: $2H_2O \\rightarrow 2H_2 + O_2$ gives 2 molecules of $H_2$ for every 1 molecule of $O_2$. The gas volumes follow this 2 : 1 ratio.\nR is false: the 2 : 1 ratio in water is by number of atoms, not by mass.\nBy mass, hydrogen : oxygen = 2 : 16 = 1 : 8.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-120","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":120,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the reaction $Fe_2O_3 + 2Al \\rightarrow Al_2O_3 + 2Fe$.\nStatement I: Aluminium is able to displace iron from iron oxide because aluminium is more reactive than iron.\nStatement II: In this reaction, iron oxide is oxidised because it takes part in the reaction with a metal.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A metal displaces another metal from its compound only if it is more reactive, and aluminium removes iron here.\nStatement II is incorrect. Iron oxide loses oxygen to become Fe, so it is reduced.\nAluminium gains oxygen to form $Al_2O_3$, so aluminium is the substance that is oxidised.\nThis makes the reaction both a displacement reaction and a redox reaction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-121","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":121,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Limestone is heated strongly in industries to obtain a substance used in the manufacture of cement. The substance is:","image":"","options":["Calcium hydroxide, $Ca(OH)_2$","Calcium carbonate, $CaCO_3$","Calcium oxide, $CaO$","Carbon dioxide, $CO_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"On heating, limestone decomposes: $CaCO_3(s) \\rightarrow CaO(s) + CO_2(g)$.\nCalcium oxide (lime or quick lime) is used in the manufacture of cement.\nCalcium hydroxide (slaked lime) is formed only when quick lime reacts with water, so it is not the product of heating limestone.\n$CO_2$ escapes as a gas, and $CaCO_3$ is the reactant.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-122","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":122,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the thermal decomposition of lead nitrate. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In the balanced equation, the coefficient of $NO_2$ is 4.\n(ii) The brown fumes observed are of oxygen.\n(iii) Lead oxide is obtained as a solid.\n(iv) The reaction absorbs energy in the form of heat.\n(v) A single reactant gives three different products.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The balanced equation is $2Pb(NO_3)_2(s) \\rightarrow 2PbO(s) + 4NO_2(g) + O_2(g)$.\n(i) Correct: the coefficient of $NO_2$ is 4.\n(ii) Incorrect: the brown fumes are $NO_2$; oxygen is colourless.\n(iii) Correct: $PbO$ is formed as a solid, shown by (s).\n(iv) Correct: heat must be supplied, so the reaction is endothermic.\n(v) Correct: the three products are $PbO$, $NO_2$ and $O_2$.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-123","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":123,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The reaction between sodium hydroxide solution and hydrochloric acid is a double displacement reaction.\nReason (R): In this reaction, the positive ions $Na^+$ and $H^+$ exchange their partner ions to form $NaCl$ and $H_2O$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The reaction is $NaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) \\rightarrow NaCl(aq) + H_2O(l)$.\nA is true: two compounds exchange ions to form two new compounds.\nR is true: $Na^+$ pairs with $Cl^-$, and $H^+$ pairs with $OH^-$ to give water.\nThis exchange of ions is exactly what makes a reaction double displacement, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-124","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":124,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In an electrolysis of water experiment, the test tube over one electrode collects 24 mL of a gas that burns with a 'pop' sound when a burning candle is brought near it. What volume of gas would be collected in the other test tube in the same time (same conditions)?","image":"","options":["12 mL","24 mL","48 mL","6 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the gas that burns with a pop is hydrogen, and its volume is 24 mL.\nRelation: from $2H_2O \\rightarrow 2H_2 + O_2$, the volume of $H_2$ : volume of $O_2$ = 2 : 1.\nSubstituting: volume of $O_2$ = 24 / 2 = 12 mL.\nSo the other tube collects 12 mL of oxygen.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-125","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":125,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student mixes a colourless solution of lead nitrate with a colourless solution of potassium iodide. Which option correctly describes the observation and the type of reaction?","image":"","options":["A yellow precipitate of lead iodide forms; double displacement reaction","A white precipitate of potassium nitrate forms; double displacement reaction","Brown fumes of nitrogen dioxide are given off; decomposition reaction","A yellow precipitate of lead iodide forms; combination reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The reaction is Pb(NO3)2(aq) + 2KI(aq) → PbI2(s) + 2KNO3(aq).\nThe ions are exchanged, so this is a double displacement (precipitation) reaction. The insoluble product, lead iodide, is yellow.\nPotassium nitrate stays dissolved, so it does not form the precipitate.\nBrown NO2 fumes appear only when solid lead nitrate is heated.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-126","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":126,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Burning of coal in air is a combination reaction.\nReason (R): In the burning of coal, carbon and oxygen combine to form a single product, carbon dioxide.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The reaction is $C(s) + O_2(g) \\rightarrow CO_2(g)$.\nTwo reactants, carbon and oxygen, form only one product, so A is true.\nR correctly states that a single product, $CO_2$, is formed.\nForming a single product is exactly what defines a combination reaction, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-127","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":127,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the electrolysis of water (Activity 1.7) and the action of sunlight on silver halides (Activity 1.8):\n(i) A few drops of dilute sulphuric acid are added to water so that it conducts electricity better.\n(ii) Both the electrolysis of water and the decomposition of silver chloride in sunlight are endothermic reactions.\n(iii) Silver bromide decomposes in sunlight in the same way as silver chloride, and this is used in black-and-white photography.\n(iv) The test tube with the smaller volume of gas makes a burning candle go off with a pop sound.\n(v) The mass of hydrogen collected is twice the mass of oxygen collected.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: pure water conducts poorly, and the acid improves conduction.\n(ii) Correct: both reactions absorb energy (electricity or light) to break down the reactant, so both are endothermic.\n(iii) Correct: $2AgBr \\rightarrow 2Ag + Br_2$ in sunlight; this is the basis of black-and-white photography.\n(iv) Incorrect: the smaller volume is oxygen. Oxygen makes the candle burn more brightly; it is hydrogen that gives the pop sound.\n(v) Incorrect: the 2 : 1 ratio is by volume. By mass, hydrogen to oxygen is 4 : 32, which is 1 : 8.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-128","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":128,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (substance oxidised).\nList-I\nA. 2PbO + C → 2Pb + CO$_2$\nB. MnO$_2$ + 4HCl → MnCl$_2$ + 2H$_2$O + Cl$_2$\nC. CuO + H$_2$ → Cu + H$_2$O\nD. 4Na + O$_2$ → 2Na$_2$O\nList-II\n(i) HCl\n(ii) Na\n(iii) C\n(iv) H$_2$","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A: Carbon gains oxygen to form CO$_2$, so C is oxidised → (iii).\nB: HCl loses hydrogen to form Cl$_2$, so HCl is oxidised → (i).\nC: H$_2$ gains oxygen to form H$_2$O, so H$_2$ is oxidised → (iv).\nD: Na gains oxygen to form Na$_2$O, so Na is oxidised → (ii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-129","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":129,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (type of reaction).\nList-I\n(a) $CaO + H_2O \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2$\n(b) $2AgBr \\rightarrow 2Ag + Br_2$ (in sunlight)\n(c) $Zn + CuSO_4 \\rightarrow ZnSO_4 + Cu$\n(d) $Na_2SO_4 + BaCl_2 \\rightarrow BaSO_4 + 2NaCl$\nList-II\n(i) Double displacement\n(ii) Combination\n(iii) Displacement\n(iv) Decomposition","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Two reactants form a single product, so it is a combination reaction (ii).\n(b) A single reactant breaks down in sunlight, so it is a decomposition reaction (iv).\n(c) Zinc removes copper from its salt, so it is a displacement reaction (iii).\n(d) The reactants exchange ions and $BaSO_4$ precipitates, so it is a double displacement reaction (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-130","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":130,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"During the electrolysis of water in Activity 1.7, the volume of gas collected in one test tube is double that in the other. The gas collected in the larger volume is:","image":"","options":["oxygen","hydrogen","sulphur dioxide","chlorine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Water decomposes as $2H_2O(l) \\rightarrow 2H_2(g) + O_2(g)$.\nTwo molecules of hydrogen form for every one molecule of oxygen, so hydrogen occupies double the volume.\nOxygen is collected in the smaller volume. Sulphur dioxide and chlorine are not produced.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-131","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":131,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (activity) with List-II (the most characteristic observation).\nList-I:\n(a) Burning magnesium ribbon in air\n(b) Mixing lead nitrate and potassium iodide solutions\n(c) Adding dilute HCl to zinc granules\n(d) Adding water to quick lime (CaO)\nList-II:\n(i) Yellow solid separates from two solutions\n(ii) Bubbles of a gas form around the solid\n(iii) Dazzling white flame and white powder\n(iv) Vigorous reaction releasing a large amount of heat","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Mg burns with a dazzling white flame and forms white MgO, so (iii).\n(b) A yellow PbI2 precipitate forms, so (i).\n(c) Hydrogen gas bubbles appear around the zinc, so (ii).\n(d) CaO reacts vigorously with water and releases a large amount of heat, so (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-132","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":132,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Solutions of barium chloride and sodium sulphate in water are mixed. Insoluble barium sulphate forms and sodium chloride remains in solution. Which equation is both balanced and has correct state symbols?","image":"","options":["$BaCl_2(aq) + Na_2SO_4(aq) \\rightarrow BaSO_4(aq) + 2NaCl(aq)$","$BaCl_2(aq) + Na_2SO_4(aq) \\rightarrow BaSO_4(s) + NaCl(aq)$","$BaCl_2(aq) + Na_2SO_4(aq) \\rightarrow BaSO_4(s) + 2NaCl(aq)$","$BaCl_2(s) + Na_2SO_4(s) \\rightarrow BaSO_4(s) + 2NaCl(s)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Both reactants are solutions, so both are (aq). The insoluble $BaSO_4$ is (s), and dissolved NaCl is (aq).\nThere are 2 Na and 2 Cl on the left, so NaCl needs the coefficient 2.\n(1) is wrong because the precipitate is shown as (aq).\n(2) has correct states but is unbalanced in Na and Cl.\n(4) shows solids, which contradicts the solution description.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-133","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":133,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following chemical equations is already balanced as written?","image":"","options":["Mg + O$_2$ → MgO","Zn + H$_2$SO$_4$ → ZnSO$_4$ + H$_2$","K + H$_2$O → KOH + H$_2$","Fe + H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + H$_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Zn + H$_2$SO$_4$ → ZnSO$_4$ + H$_2$ has Zn 1, H 2, S 1 and O 4 on both sides, so it is balanced.\nMg + O$_2$ → MgO has 2 O atoms on the left but only 1 on the right.\nK + H$_2$O → KOH + H$_2$ has 2 H atoms on the left but 3 on the right.\nFe + H$_2$O → Fe$_3$O$_4$ + H$_2$ has 1 Fe on the left but 3 on the right.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-134","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":134,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Corrosion damages car bodies, bridges and ships.","Corrosion of iron is a serious problem, and a lot of money is spent every year to replace damaged iron.","Rusting of iron is a reduction process in which iron loses oxygen.","Fats and oils become rancid when they are oxidised."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT lists car bodies, bridges, railings and ships as objects damaged by corrosion.\n(2) Correct: replacing corroded iron costs an enormous amount of money every year.\n(3) Incorrect: in rusting, iron combines with oxygen. Gaining oxygen is oxidation, not reduction.\n(4) Correct: rancidity is caused by the oxidation of fats and oils.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-135","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":135,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The reaction $Ca(OH)_2 + CO_2 \\rightarrow CaCO_3 + H_2O$ that occurs on whitewashed walls is a combination reaction.\nStatement II: The compound formed on the walls in this reaction has the same chemical formula as marble.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. This reaction gives two products, $CaCO_3$ and $H_2O$. A combination reaction must give a single product.\nStatement II is correct. The compound formed is calcium carbonate, $CaCO_3$, and marble also has the formula $CaCO_3$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-136","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":136,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following reactions is endothermic?","image":"","options":["Burning of natural gas, $CH_4 + 2O_2 \\rightarrow CO_2 + 2H_2O$","Respiration of glucose in body cells","Heating of limestone, $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$","Reaction of quick lime with water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Limestone decomposes only when it is heated continuously. Heat energy is absorbed to break it into CaO and $CO_2$, so the reaction is endothermic.\nBurning of natural gas, respiration and the reaction of quick lime with water all give out heat, so they are exothermic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-137","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":137,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the equation $3Fe(s) + 4H_2O(g) \\rightarrow Fe_3O_4(s) + 4H_2(g)$, the symbol (g) with water shows that water is used as steam.\nStatement II: The symbol (aq) in a chemical equation means that the substance is present as a pure liquid.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT writes $H_2O(g)$ in this reaction because iron reacts with steam.\nStatement II is incorrect. (aq) means the substance is present as a solution in water. A pure liquid is shown by (l).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-138","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":138,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Hydrogen gas is passed over heated copper powder that has a black coating. The black coating turns brown. Which statement correctly explains this change?","image":"","options":["Copper oxide gains hydrogen and is oxidised","Hydrogen is reduced to water","Copper is oxidised further to a brown oxide","Copper(II) oxide loses oxygen and is reduced to copper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The reaction is CuO + H$_2$ → Cu + H$_2$O.\nCuO loses oxygen, so it is reduced. The brown colour comes from the copper metal formed.\nHydrogen gains oxygen to form water, so hydrogen is oxidised, not reduced.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-139","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":139,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Manufacturers of potato chips flush the packets with nitrogen gas before sealing them.\nReason (R): Nitrogen combines chemically with the fats and oils in chips and makes them resistant to rancidity.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that chips bags are flushed with nitrogen to prevent the chips from getting oxidised.\nR is false. Nitrogen does not react with the fats.\nNitrogen works because it is unreactive and pushes out the oxygen, which slows down the oxidation of fats and oils.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-140","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":140,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are combination reactions?\n(i) $2H_2 + O_2 \\rightarrow 2H_2O$\n(ii) $H_2 + Cl_2 \\rightarrow 2HCl$\n(iii) $2Mg + O_2 \\rightarrow 2MgO$\n(iv) $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$\n(v) $Ca(OH)_2 + CO_2 \\rightarrow CaCO_3 + H_2O$\n(vi) $C + O_2 \\rightarrow CO_2$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Combination: the only product is water.\n(ii) Combination: the only product is HCl.\n(iii) Combination: the only product is MgO.\n(iv) Not combination: one reactant breaks into two products, so it is a decomposition.\n(v) Not combination: two products, $CaCO_3$ and $H_2O$, are formed.\n(vi) Combination: the only product is $CO_2$.\nSo the total is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-141","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":141,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the decomposition of silver halides by light are correct?\n(i) White silver chloride turns grey on exposure to sunlight.\n(ii) Chlorine gas is released when silver chloride decomposes in sunlight.\n(iii) The decomposition of silver chloride in sunlight is an exothermic reaction.\n(iv) Silver bromide decomposes in sunlight in a similar way, giving silver and bromine.\n(v) These reactions are used in black-and-white photography.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: grey silver metal is formed.\n(ii) Correct: $2AgCl \\rightarrow 2Ag + Cl_2$.\n(iii) Incorrect: light energy is absorbed, so the reaction is endothermic, not exothermic.\n(iv) Correct: $2AgBr \\rightarrow 2Ag + Br_2$.\n(v) Correct: NCERT states this use.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-142","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":142,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following does the reaction mixture become cold, showing that energy is absorbed?","image":"","options":["Adding water slowly to calcium oxide (quick lime) in a beaker","Burning methane (natural gas) in air","Mixing barium hydroxide with ammonium chloride in a test tube","Oxidation of glucose in the cells of our body"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Reactions that absorb energy are endothermic, and those that release heat are exothermic.\n(1) CaO + H$_2$O gives slaked lime and releases a large amount of heat, so it is exothermic.\n(2) CH$_4$ + 2O$_2$ → CO$_2$ + 2H$_2$O releases heat, so it is exothermic.\n(3) Ba(OH)$_2$ mixed with NH$_4$Cl takes in heat, so the bottom of the test tube feels cold. This reaction is endothermic.\n(4) Respiration releases energy, so it is exothermic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-143","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":143,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the word-equation: Magnesium + Oxygen → Magnesium oxide\nWhich statement about it is correct?","image":"","options":["Magnesium oxide is a reactant, written on the left-hand side.","The arrowhead points towards the products and shows the direction of the reaction.","Magnesium and oxygen are products because they appear first.","The plus sign shows that magnesium and oxygen are mixed without any change."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a word-equation, the reactants (magnesium, oxygen) are written on the LHS and joined by a plus sign.\nThe product (magnesium oxide) is written on the RHS.\nThe arrowhead points towards the products and shows the direction of the reaction.\nThe plus sign only separates the reactants. It does not mean they stay unchanged.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-144","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":144,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the unbalanced equation Fe + H2O → Fe3O4 + H2.\nStatement I: To balance the oxygen atoms, water may be written as (H2O)4 or H2O4.\nStatement II: In the correctly balanced equation with the smallest whole-number coefficients, the number of H2 molecules formed equals the number of H2O molecules used.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: formulae must never be altered while balancing. The correct way is to place the coefficient 4 in front of water, as 4H2O.\nThe hit-and-trial method then gives 3Fe + 4H2O → Fe3O4 + 4H2.\nStatement II is correct: 4 molecules of H2O are used and 4 molecules of H2 are formed. This keeps 8 H atoms on each side.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-145","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":145,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Limestone is heated strongly to obtain quick lime (Step 1). The quick lime is then mixed with water to make a solution used for whitewashing (Step 2). Which option correctly describes the two steps?","image":"","options":["Step 1: combination, exothermic; Step 2: decomposition, endothermic","Step 1: decomposition, exothermic; Step 2: combination, endothermic","Step 1: decomposition, endothermic; Step 2: combination, endothermic","Step 1: decomposition, endothermic; Step 2: combination, exothermic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$ on heating. A single reactant breaks down, so it is a thermal decomposition. Heat is absorbed, so it is endothermic.\nStep 2: $CaO + H_2O \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2$ + heat. Two reactants form a single product, so it is a combination reaction. Heat is released, so it is exothermic.\nOption (3) is tempting, but quick lime reacting with water gives out a large amount of heat, not absorbs it.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-146","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":146,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A strip of lead kept in copper chloride solution gets coated with copper.\nReason (R): Lead is a less reactive metal than copper.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: Pb(s) + CuCl$_2$(aq) → PbCl$_2$(aq) + Cu(s). Copper is displaced and deposits on the lead strip.\nR is false: NCERT states that lead is more reactive than copper. That is why lead can displace copper.\nIf lead were less reactive than copper, no displacement would occur. So R contradicts A rather than explaining it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-147","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":147,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the refining of silver, silver is recovered from silver nitrate solution by adding copper metal:\n$Cu + 2AgNO_3 \\rightarrow Cu(NO_3)_2 + 2Ag$\nWhich of the following statements about this reaction is correct?","image":"","options":["Silver displaces copper from copper nitrate","Silver is more reactive than copper","Two atoms of silver are set free for every atom of copper that reacts","It is a double displacement reaction because two salts are involved"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: copper is the free metal and displaces silver, not the other way round.\n(2) Incorrect: the more reactive metal does the displacing, so copper is more reactive than silver.\n(3) Correct: the balanced equation shows 1 Cu giving 2 Ag. This is because copper nitrate holds two nitrate groups while silver nitrate holds one.\n(4) Incorrect: a free element replaces another element, so it is a single displacement reaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-148","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":148,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Sodium hydroxide solution (in water) reacts with hydrochloric acid solution (in water) to give sodium chloride solution and water. Which equation represents this correctly with state symbols?","image":"","options":["$NaOH(s) + HCl(aq) \\rightarrow NaCl(s) + H_2O(l)$","$NaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) \\rightarrow NaCl(aq) + H_2O(aq)$","$NaOH(aq) + HCl(g) \\rightarrow NaCl(aq) + H_2O(g)$","$NaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) \\rightarrow NaCl(aq) + H_2O(l)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NaOH, HCl and NaCl are all present as solutions in water, so each gets (aq).\nWater is a liquid, so it is written $H_2O(l)$, not (aq).\nOption (2) wrongly gives water the symbol (aq).\nOptions (1) and (3) show solid or gaseous states that do not match the description.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-149","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":149,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student performs the following tests and records the temperature in each beaker before and after adding the solid.\nBeaker B: 25 mL water + ammonium nitrate, 25 °C to 18 °C\nBeaker C: 25 mL water + anhydrous copper sulphate, 25 °C to 31 °C\nBeaker D: 25 mL copper sulphate solution + iron filings, 25 °C to 27 °C\nConsider the following conclusions.\n(i) The process in beaker B absorbs heat, so it is endothermic.\n(ii) The process in beaker C releases heat, so it is exothermic.\n(iii) In beaker D, the blue colour fades and a brownish coating of copper forms, because iron displaces copper.\n(iv) In beaker D, a double displacement reaction occurs as ions are exchanged between two compounds.\nWhich of these conclusions are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the temperature falls, so heat is absorbed from the surroundings. The process is endothermic.\n(ii) Correct: the temperature rises, so heat is given out. The process is exothermic.\n(iii) Correct: $Fe + CuSO_4 \\to FeSO_4 + Cu$. The blue colour fades and copper deposits on the iron.\n(iv) Incorrect: an element (Fe) replaces another element (Cu). This is a single displacement, not an exchange of ions between two compounds.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-150","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":150,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Heating limestone to form quick lime and carbon dioxide is a thermal decomposition reaction.\nReason (R): Quick lime is used in the manufacture of cement.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. In $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$, a single reactant breaks down into simpler products. Because the energy is supplied as heat, it is a thermal decomposition.\nR is true. NCERT states that one use of quick lime is in making cement.\nHowever, R only describes a use of the product. It does not explain why the reaction is called a thermal decomposition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-151","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":151,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Manufacturers fill packets of potato chips with nitrogen gas before sealing them. What is the main purpose of doing this?","image":"","options":["To add a pleasant flavour to the chips","To keep oxygen away so that the oil in the chips does not get oxidised","To kill germs by reacting with them","To make the chips absorb nitrogen and become crispier"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chips contain oil. When the oil reacts with oxygen it becomes rancid, and its smell and taste change.\nNitrogen fills the packet and pushes out the air, so very little oxygen is left to oxidise the oil.\nNitrogen does not add flavour or react with the chips. It simply keeps oxygen away.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-152","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":152,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student slowly adds water to a small amount of calcium oxide in a beaker and touches the beaker. Which observation and conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["Beaker becomes cold; endothermic combination reaction","Beaker becomes hot; exothermic combination reaction","Beaker becomes hot; exothermic decomposition reaction","No temperature change; physical change only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The reaction is $CaO(s) + H_2O(l) \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2(aq) + Heat$.\nA large amount of heat is released, so the beaker feels hot. This makes the reaction exothermic.\nTwo reactants form a single product, so it is a combination reaction, not a decomposition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-153","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":153,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In Activity 1.9, two cleaned iron nails are kept in copper sulphate solution for about 20 minutes. A third cleaned nail is kept aside for comparison. Which observation is expected when the dipped nails are taken out?","image":"","options":["The dipped nails become brownish, and the blue colour of the solution fades","The dipped nails stay shiny, and the solution turns a deeper blue","The dipped nails get a white coating, and the solution turns brown","The dipped nails look like the nail kept aside, and the solution stays unchanged"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Reaction: Fe(s) + CuSO$_4$(aq) → FeSO$_4$(aq) + Cu(s).\nIron is more reactive than copper, so it displaces copper from copper sulphate.\nThe copper set free deposits on the nails, so they look brownish.\nAs Cu$^{2+}$ ions are used up, the blue colour of the solution fades. Iron sulphate forms instead.\nA deeper blue colour or an unchanged nail would mean no reaction took place, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-154","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":154,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match each reaction in List-I with the substance that is oxidised in it, given in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) $ZnO + C \\rightarrow Zn + CO$\n(B) $4Na + O_2 \\rightarrow 2Na_2O$\n(C) $CuO + H_2 \\rightarrow Cu + H_2O$\n(D) $MnO_2 + 4HCl \\rightarrow MnCl_2 + 2H_2O + Cl_2$\nList-II\n(i) HCl\n(ii) C\n(iii) Na\n(iv) $H_2$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Carbon gains oxygen and becomes CO, so C is oxidised: A-(ii).\n(B) Sodium gains oxygen and becomes $Na_2O$, so Na is oxidised: B-(iii).\n(C) Hydrogen gains oxygen and becomes $H_2O$, so $H_2$ is oxidised: C-(iv).\n(D) HCl loses hydrogen and becomes $Cl_2$, so HCl is oxidised: D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-155","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":155,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student observes the following:\n• A strip of metal P placed in a solution of a salt of metal Q gets coated with metal Q.\n• A strip of metal R placed in a solution of a salt of metal P gets coated with metal P.\nWhich of the following reactions is also expected to occur?","image":"","options":["Metal Q in a solution of a salt of P","Metal P in a solution of a salt of R","Metal Q in a solution of a salt of R","Metal R in a solution of a salt of Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A metal displaces another only if it is more reactive.\nP displaces Q, so P is more reactive than Q.\nR displaces P, so R is more reactive than P.\nThe order of reactivity is therefore R > P > Q.\nOnly R placed in a salt of Q has a more reactive metal displacing a less reactive one.\nIn options (1), (2) and (3), the free metal is the less reactive one, so no reaction occurs.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-156","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":156,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Hydrogen sulphide gas burns in air to give water and sulphur dioxide. When this equation is balanced using the smallest whole-number coefficients, how many oxygen atoms are present on the product side?","image":"","options":["3","4","6","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Skeletal equation: H$_2$S + O$_2$ → H$_2$O + SO$_2$.\nH and S balance one-to-one, but the products contain 3 O atoms for each H$_2$S. Doubling everything removes the fraction: 2H$_2$S + 3O$_2$ → 2H$_2$O + 2SO$_2$.\nOxygen on the product side: 2 (in 2H$_2$O) + 4 (in 2SO$_2$) = 6. This matches the 3O$_2$ (6 atoms) on the left.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-157","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":157,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The decomposition of silver chloride in sunlight is an endothermic reaction.\nReason (R): Decomposition reactions require energy in the form of heat, light or electricity to break down the reactant.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: silver chloride absorbs light energy in order to decompose, so the reaction is endothermic.\nR is true: NCERT states that decomposition reactions need energy as heat, light or electricity.\nR explains A, because the energy absorbed from light is exactly what makes the reaction endothermic.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-158","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":158,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following observations show that a chemical reaction has taken place?\n(i) A yellow precipitate forms when potassium iodide solution is added to lead nitrate solution.\n(ii) Gas bubbles appear when dilute sulphuric acid is added to zinc granules.\n(iii) The beaker becomes hot when water is added to quick lime.\n(iv) The total mass of substances inside a sealed flask increases after the reaction.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Valid: a change in colour and the formation of a precipitate show a reaction.\n(ii) Valid: the evolution of a gas (hydrogen) shows a reaction.\n(iii) Valid: a change in temperature shows a reaction.\n(iv) Invalid: by the law of conservation of mass, total mass in a closed system stays the same, so such an increase cannot happen.\nThree observations are valid.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-159","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":159,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Barium chloride reacts with potassium sulphate to give barium sulphate and potassium chloride. Which is the correctly balanced equation?","image":"","options":["$BaCl_2 + K_2SO_4 \\rightarrow BaSO_4 + KCl$","$BaCl_2 + K_2SO_4 \\rightarrow BaSO_4 + 2KCl$","$BaCl_2 + 2K_2SO_4 \\rightarrow BaSO_4 + 2KCl$","$2BaCl_2 + K_2SO_4 \\rightarrow 2BaSO_4 + 2KCl$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The LHS has 2 K atoms and 2 Cl atoms, so 2 KCl molecules are needed on the RHS.\nCheck each element: Ba is 1 = 1, S is 1 = 1, O is 4 = 4, K is 2 = 2 and Cl is 2 = 2.\nOption (1) leaves K and Cl unbalanced.\nOptions (3) and (4) give unequal numbers of S, O or Ba atoms on the two sides.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-160","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":160,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student cleans three identical iron nails and keeps them for a week as follows:\nNail P: coated with paint and kept in a humid room\nNail Q: left uncoated in the same humid room\nNail R: left uncoated inside a tightly closed jar containing dry air\nWhich nail will show the most reddish-brown coating at the end of the week, and why?","image":"","options":["Nail P, because paint reacts with iron","Nail Q, because it is exposed to both moisture and air","Nail R, because air is trapped around it","All three equally, because rusting depends only on time"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The reddish-brown coating is rust. It forms when iron is attacked by the moisture and air around it.\nP: the paint keeps moisture and air away from the iron, so it rusts very little.\nR: the jar has air but almost no moisture, so it rusts very little.\nQ: it is exposed to both moisture and air, so it rusts the most.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-161","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":161,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following equations are correctly balanced?\n(i) N$_2$ + 3H$_2$ → 2NH$_3$\n(ii) 2H$_2$S + 3O$_2$ → 2H$_2$O + 2SO$_2$\n(iii) K + H$_2$O → KOH + H$_2$\n(iv) 2NaOH + H$_2$SO$_4$ → Na$_2$SO$_4$ + 2H$_2$O\n(v) HNO$_3$ + Ca(OH)$_2$ → Ca(NO$_3$)$_2$ + 2H$_2$O","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Balanced: N 2 = 2, H 6 = 6.\n(ii) Balanced: H 4 = 4, S 2 = 2, O 6 = 6.\n(iii) Not balanced: H is 2 on the left and 3 on the right. The correct form is 2K + 2H$_2$O → 2KOH + H$_2$.\n(iv) Balanced: Na 2, S 1, O 6 and H 4 on each side.\n(v) Not balanced: N is 1 on the left and 2 on the right. HNO$_3$ needs the coefficient 2.\nSo 3 equations are balanced.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-162","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":162,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"While balancing $Fe + H_2O \\rightarrow Fe_3O_4 + H_2$ by the hit-and-trial method, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It is convenient to start with $Fe_3O_4$ and balance oxygen first, because it is the compound with the most atoms.","To balance oxygen, $H_2O$ can be rewritten as $H_2O_4$ on the reactant side.","The balanced equation has the coefficients 3, 4, 1 and 4 in the order written.","After oxygen is balanced, hydrogen is balanced by taking 4 molecules of $H_2$ on the product side."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: begin with the compound having the most atoms, and the element with the most atoms in it (O in $Fe_3O_4$).\n(2) Incorrect: formulae must never be changed. Oxygen is balanced only by putting a coefficient, as $4H_2O$.\n(3) Correct: $3Fe + 4H_2O \\rightarrow Fe_3O_4 + 4H_2$.\n(4) Correct: $4H_2O$ gives 8 H atoms, so 4 molecules of $H_2$ are needed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-163","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":163,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match the substance heated in List-I with the solid product obtained in List-II.\nList-I\n(P) Ferrous sulphate\n(Q) Calcium carbonate\n(R) Lead nitrate\n(S) Zinc carbonate\nList-II\n(i) $ZnO$\n(ii) $PbO$\n(iii) $Fe_2O_3$\n(iv) $CaO$","image":"","options":["P-(iii), Q-(iv), R-(ii), S-(i)","P-(iii), Q-(i), R-(ii), S-(iv)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(iii), S-(i)","P-(iv), Q-(iii), R-(i), S-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P: $2FeSO_4 \\rightarrow Fe_2O_3 + SO_2 + SO_3$, so P matches (iii) $Fe_2O_3$ (ferric oxide).\nQ: $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$, so Q matches (iv) $CaO$.\nR: $2Pb(NO_3)_2 \\rightarrow 2PbO + 4NO_2 + O_2$, so R matches (ii) $PbO$.\nS: $ZnCO_3 \\rightarrow ZnO + CO_2$, so S matches (i) $ZnO$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-164","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":164,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are displacement reactions (not double displacement)?\n(i) $Fe_2O_3 + 2Al \\rightarrow Al_2O_3 + 2Fe$\n(ii) $Na_2SO_4 + BaCl_2 \\rightarrow BaSO_4 + 2NaCl$\n(iii) $Mg + 2HCl \\rightarrow MgCl_2 + H_2$\n(iv) $2Al + 3CuCl_2 \\rightarrow 2AlCl_3 + 3Cu$\n(v) $Zn + 2AgNO_3 \\rightarrow Zn(NO_3)_2 + 2Ag$\n(vi) $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Displacement: Al removes Fe from its oxide.\n(ii) Double displacement: the ions are exchanged and a BaSO$_4$ precipitate forms.\n(iii) Displacement: Mg displaces hydrogen from HCl, giving H$_2$ and MgCl$_2$.\n(iv) Displacement: the more reactive Al displaces Cu.\n(v) Displacement: Zn displaces the less reactive Ag.\n(vi) Thermal decomposition, not displacement.\nTotal displacement reactions = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-165","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":165,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When green ferrous sulphate crystals are heated, their colour changes because the crystals lose water of crystallisation.\nStatement II: On heating, lead nitrate decomposes to give only colourless gases.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $FeSO_4 \\cdot 7H_2O$ first loses water on heating, and its green colour changes. It then decomposes further.\nStatement II is incorrect. Lead nitrate gives brown $NO_2$ along with colourless $O_2$, so not all the gases are colourless.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-166","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":166,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In Activity 1.9, iron nails are left in copper sulphate solution for about 20 minutes. The blue colour of the solution fades. Which of the following best explains this?","image":"","options":["Iron displaces copper from copper sulphate, so blue copper sulphate is replaced by iron sulphate","Copper displaces iron from the nail, so the solution gets diluted","Iron nails absorb water from the solution and copper sulphate crystallises out","Copper sulphate decomposes into copper and sulphur dioxide on contact with iron"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The reaction is $Fe(s) + CuSO_4(aq) \\rightarrow FeSO_4(aq) + Cu(s)$.\nIron is more reactive than copper, so it removes copper from copper sulphate.\nThe blue copper sulphate is used up and iron sulphate forms in its place, so the blue colour fades. The displaced copper deposits on the nail as a brownish coating.\nOption (2) reverses the roles: copper cannot displace the more reactive iron.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-167","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":167,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When sodium sulphate and barium chloride solutions are mixed, the $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ ions combine to form the white precipitate.\nStatement II: The white precipitate in this reaction is formed by the combination of $Ba^{2+}$ and $SO_4^{2-}$ ions.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ form sodium chloride, which stays dissolved in the solution.\nStatement II is correct: $Ba^{2+}$ and $SO_4^{2-}$ ions combine to form insoluble $BaSO_4$, the white precipitate.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-168","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":168,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are double displacement reactions?\n(i) $Pb(NO_3)_2(aq) + 2KI(aq) \\rightarrow PbI_2(s) + 2KNO_3(aq)$\n(ii) $Zn(s) + CuSO_4(aq) \\rightarrow ZnSO_4(aq) + Cu(s)$\n(iii) $NaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) \\rightarrow NaCl(aq) + H_2O(l)$\n(iv) $BaCl_2(aq) + H_2SO_4(aq) \\rightarrow BaSO_4(s) + 2HCl(aq)$\n(v) $2Pb(NO_3)_2(s) \\xrightarrow{Heat} 2PbO(s) + 4NO_2(g) + O_2(g)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: the two compounds exchange ions and yellow $PbI_2$ precipitates.\n(ii) No: a single element, zinc, displaces copper from its compound. This is a displacement reaction.\n(iii) Yes: $Na^{+}$ and $H^{+}$ exchange partners to give $NaCl$ and $H_2O$.\n(iv) Yes: the ions are exchanged and white $BaSO_4$ precipitates.\n(v) No: a single reactant breaks down on heating. This is thermal decomposition.\nSo three reactions, (i), (iii) and (iv), are double displacement reactions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-169","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":169,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Decomposition reactions need energy in the form of heat, light or electricity to break down the reactant","The burning of coal, $C + O_2 \\rightarrow CO_2$, is both a combination reaction and an exothermic reaction","All decomposition reactions release heat because a single reactant breaks into simpler products","Reactions in which energy is absorbed are called endothermic reactions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT states that decomposition reactions need heat, light or electricity.\n(2) Correct: carbon and oxygen form a single product, and heat is given out.\n(3) Incorrect: decomposition reactions absorb energy, so they are endothermic. Breaking a reactant into simpler products does not release heat.\n(4) Correct: this is the definition of an endothermic reaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-170","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":170,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Iron gates and railings are painted to protect them from rusting.\nReason (R): A coat of paint stops moisture and air from reaching the surface of the iron.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Painting is a common way to protect iron articles from rusting.\nR is true. Rusting needs moisture and air, and paint forms a layer that keeps both away from the iron.\nSince R states exactly why paint prevents rusting, R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-171","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":171,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the reaction $MnO_2 + 4HCl \\rightarrow MnCl_2 + 2H_2O + Cl_2$. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["$MnO_2$ is oxidised because it gains chlorine.","HCl is reduced because it forms water.","HCl is oxidised to $Cl_2$ by losing hydrogen, while $MnO_2$ is reduced by losing oxygen.","It is not a redox reaction, because no substance gains oxygen."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"HCl changes to $Cl_2$, so it loses hydrogen. Loss of hydrogen is oxidation.\n$MnO_2$ changes to $MnCl_2$, so it loses oxygen. Loss of oxygen is reduction.\nOne substance is oxidised and the other reduced, so this is a redox reaction. Option (4) is therefore wrong.\nOxidation can also be identified by loss of hydrogen, not only by gain of oxygen.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-172","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":172,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) When copper powder is heated in air, its surface becomes coated with black copper(II) oxide.\n(ii) When hydrogen is passed over the heated black coating, it turns brown again.\n(iii) In $CuO + H_2 \\rightarrow Cu + H_2O$, hydrogen is reduced because it combines with oxygen.\n(iv) Fats and oils become rancid because they are oxidised.\n(v) Chips packets are flushed with nitrogen because nitrogen speeds up the oxidation of oils.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $2Cu + O_2 \\rightarrow 2CuO$ forms a black coating.\n(ii) Correct: CuO is reduced back to brown copper.\n(iii) Incorrect: hydrogen gains oxygen, so it is oxidised. CuO is the substance reduced.\n(iv) Correct: rancidity is caused by the oxidation of fats and oils.\n(v) Incorrect: nitrogen is used to prevent oxidation, not to speed it up.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-173","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":173,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Potassium iodide solution is added to lead nitrate solution in a test tube. What is observed?","image":"","options":["A white precipitate of lead chloride forms.","Brown fumes of nitrogen dioxide are evolved.","A yellow precipitate of lead iodide forms.","The solution turns blue and no solid appears."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Both reactants are colourless solutions.\nWhen they are mixed, a yellow insoluble solid separates out. This solid is lead iodide (PbI2).\nBrown NO2 fumes appear only when solid lead nitrate is heated, not in this solution reaction.\nNo chloride is present, so lead chloride cannot form.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-174","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":174,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the balanced equation 3Fe(s) + 4H$_2$O(g) → Fe$_3$O$_4$(s) + 4H$_2$(g). Which of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The total number of atoms is 15 on each side.\n(ii) The total mass of the reactants equals the total mass of the products.\n(iii) The total number of formula units/molecules is the same on both sides.\n(iv) The symbol (g) with H$_2$O shows that water reacts in the form of steam.","image":"","options":["(i) and (iii) only","(ii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the left side has 3 + 4×3 = 15 atoms. The right side has 7 + 4×2 = 15 atoms.\n(ii) Correct: equal atoms on both sides means equal mass (conservation of mass).\n(iii) Incorrect: the left side has 3 + 4 = 7 units, but the right side has 1 + 4 = 5. Molecules need not be conserved.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT states that (g) with H$_2$O means steam.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-175","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":175,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student says that a skeletal chemical equation must always be balanced before it is accepted as correct. Which law is the main reason for this?","image":"","options":["Law of constant proportions","Law of conservation of mass","Law of multiple proportions","Law of conservation of energy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.\nSo the number of atoms of each element must be the same before and after the reaction.\nAn unbalanced equation shows different masses on the two sides, which breaks the law of conservation of mass.\nConservation of energy deals with energy, not with counting atoms, so it is not the reason.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-176","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":176,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In a chemical equation, the notation (aq) written after the formula of a substance indicates that the substance is","image":"","options":["present as a solution in water","present in the liquid state as a pure substance","present in the form of steam","dissolved in any liquid solvent such as alcohol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT uses (aq), meaning aqueous, when a reactant or product is present as a solution in water.\nA pure liquid, such as water itself, is shown as (l), so option (2) is wrong.\nSteam is shown as (g).\nThe term aqueous refers only to water as the solvent, not to any solvent.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-177","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":177,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Case: A shiny brown metal X is heated in air and turns into a black compound Y. A colourless gas Z is then passed over heated Y. The black solid turns brown again, and droplets of a colourless liquid collect on the cooler part of the tube. Which option correctly identifies Y and Z and their roles in the second step?","image":"","options":["Y is CuO and is reduced; Z is hydrogen and is oxidised","Y is CuO and is oxidised; Z is hydrogen and is reduced","Y is iron oxide and is reduced; Z is oxygen and is oxidised","Y is CuO and Z is hydrogen; both are reduced as water forms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A shiny brown metal that turns black on heating in air is copper, so X = Cu and Y = CuO.\nThe brown colour returning, along with liquid droplets forming, shows CuO + H$_2$ → Cu + H$_2$O. So Z is hydrogen and the liquid is water.\nCuO loses oxygen and is reduced. H$_2$ gains oxygen and is oxidised.\nIn a redox reaction one substance is oxidised while the other is reduced, so both cannot be reduced.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-178","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":178,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Green ferrous sulphate crystals are heated strongly in a dry boiling tube. The colour changes, and a smell of burning sulphur is noticed.\nConsider these statements:\n(i) The reaction is a thermal decomposition.\n(ii) It forms one solid product and two gaseous products.\n(iii) The crystals first lose their water of crystallisation, which changes their colour.\n(iv) The decomposition is exothermic, because a smell is given off.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(i) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The reaction is 2FeSO4 → Fe2O3 + SO2 + SO3.\n(i) Correct: a single reactant breaks down on heating.\n(ii) Correct: Fe2O3 is a solid, while SO2 and SO3 are gases.\n(iii) Correct: FeSO4·7H2O loses water when heated, and its colour changes.\n(iv) Incorrect: the decomposition needs heat, so it is endothermic. A smell does not show that heat is released.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-179","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":179,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, a substance is said to be oxidised in a reaction when it:","image":"","options":["loses oxygen or gains hydrogen","gains oxygen or gains hydrogen","gains oxygen or loses hydrogen","loses oxygen or loses hydrogen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines oxidation as the gain of oxygen or the loss of hydrogen.\nReduction is the opposite: the loss of oxygen or the gain of hydrogen.\nOption (1) describes reduction. Options (2) and (4) mix one oxidation criterion with one reduction criterion.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE10-SCI-01-180","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Reactions and Equations","qno":180,"subject":"Science","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A student adds potassium iodide solution to a test tube containing colourless lead nitrate solution. Which observation shows that a chemical reaction has taken place?","image":"","options":["A white precipitate forms and the test tube becomes cold","Brown fumes are given off from the mouth of the test tube","A yellow precipitate forms in the test tube","The solution turns blue and a reddish-brown solid settles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Lead nitrate and potassium iodide react to form lead iodide.\nLead iodide is a yellow solid that does not dissolve in water, so it appears as a yellow precipitate.\nThe change in colour and the formation of a new solid show that a chemical reaction has occurred.\nBrown fumes of $NO_2$ are seen only when solid lead nitrate is heated, not when the two solutions are mixed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 10 Science, Ch 1 · Chemical Reactions and Equations","difficulty":"easy"}]});
